st \\(m\\) vertical lines replaced by the sunny lines \\(s_1,\\dots ,s_m\\)) still covers the whole \\(T_n\\) for every \\(m\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n4. **Counting sunny lines.**  \n   In the family \\(\\mathcal S_m\\) exactly the lines \\(s_1,\\dots ,s_m\\) are sunny, while the remaining \\(n-m\\) lines are vertical (non‑sunny).  \n   Therefore \\(\\mathcal S_m\\) realises \\(k=m\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   By choosing \\(m=k\\) we obtain, for each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), a collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Hence every integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) is admissible, and no other integer can occur because the total number of lines is exactly \\(n\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For a given }n\\ge 3\\text{ the possible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,2,\\dots ,n.}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nN_n=|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A family with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the vertical line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;x=i .\n\\]\n\nThe lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) are distinct, none of them is sunny, and  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i\\cap T_n=\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}\\bigl(\\ell_i\\cap T_n\\bigr)=T_n .\n\\]\n\nThus the required configuration exists for \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A sunny line that replaces a vertical line\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) consider  \n\n\\[\ns_i:\\; y=\\frac12(x-i)+\\frac12 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(s_i\\) is \\(1/2\\); hence \\(s_i\\) is sunny.  \nThe line \\(s_i\\) passes through the two lattice points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,1),\\qquad Q_i=(i+1,2).\n\\]\n\nBecause the two points are consecutive on the diagonal \\(a+b= i+2\\), the whole segment of \\(s_i\\) that lies inside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) consists of the points  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i+j-1,j)\\mid j\\ge 1,\\;i+j-1\\le n,\\;j\\le n+2-i\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIn particular, for every \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n+1-i\\) the point \\((i,j)\\) (which belongs to the vertical line \\(\\ell_i\\)) also belongs to \\(s_i\\) **except** the highest point \\((i,n+2-i)\\) of that column.  \nThat exceptional point is contained in the line \\(s_{i-1}\\) (or in \\(s_n\\) when \\(i=1\\)), because  \n\n\\[\ns_{i-1}\\ni (i,n+2-i)\\qquad (2\\le i\\le n).\n\\]\n\nHence the union of the two consecutive sunny lines \\(s_{i-1}\\) and \\(s_i\\) covers the whole column \\(x=i\\) of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Replacing several vertical lines\n\nFix an integer \\(m\\) with \\(0\\le m\\le n\\).  \nDefine the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_m=\n\\underbrace{\\{s_1,s_2,\\dots ,s_m\\}}_{\\text{sunny}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{\\ell_{m+1},\\ell_{m+2},\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}}_{\\text{vertical}} .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe lines in \\(\\mathcal S_m\\) are distinct because the slopes of the \\(s_i\\) are all equal to \\(1/2\\) but their intercepts differ, while each \\(\\ell_j\\) is vertical.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nFor any column \\(x=i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le m\\) the points of that column are covered by the two sunny lines \\(s_{i-1}\\) (if \\(i\\ge 2\\)) and \\(s_i\\); for \\(i=1\\) they are covered by \\(s_1\\) and \\(s_n\\).  \nFor a column \\(x=i\\) with \\(m+1\\le i\\le n\\) the points are covered by the vertical line \\(\\ell_i\\).  \nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal S_m\\).\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nExactly the lines \\(s_1,\\dots ,s_m\\) are sunny, so \\(\\mathcal S_m\\) contains precisely \\(k=m\\) sunny lines.\n\n----------------