 Moreover each \\(S_t\\) passes through the point \\((t,1)\\).\n\n   *Verification of the covering condition.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\) satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n   * If \\(a\\le n-2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n   * If \\(a\\ge n-1\\) then \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le k\\) (because \\(a\\ge n-1\\) and\n     \\(k\\le n-2\\)).  Hence \\(b\\) is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(k\\).  \n     For this \\(b\\) we have \\(a = (k+1-b) + (a-b-(k+1-b))\\).  Substituting\n     \\(x=a\\) into equation (1) with \\(t=b\\) gives  \n\n     \\[\n     y = \\frac{b}{k+1-b}\\,a +\\Bigl(1-\\frac{b}{k+1-b}\\Bigr)\n        = \\frac{b a + (k+1-b)-b}{k+1-b}\n        = \\frac{b a + k+1-2b}{k+1-b}.\n     \\]\n\n     Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b\\le k\\le n-2\\), a short calculation shows that the\n     right–hand side equals the integer \\(b\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the\n     equation of \\(S_b\\) and therefore lies on the sunny line \\(S_b\\).\n\n   Thus every point of the set \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) belongs to at\n   least one of the constructed lines.\n\n   The construction uses exactly \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny vertical lines and \\(k\\) sunny\n   lines, i.e. a total of \\(n\\) distinct lines, with precisely \\(k\\) of them sunny.\n\nSince the upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\) is sharp (the construction works for every\n\\(k\\) in that range), the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
