ust lie on a sunny line.  Two distinct\nsunny lines intersect at most once, and none of them is vertical; consequently\neach sunny line can contain at most one point of each vertical line\n\\(x=d\\) with \\(d\\neq c\\).\n\nThe set \\(S_n\\) contains the points \\((1,1),(2,1),\\dots ,(n-1,1)\\) on the\nhorizontal line \\(y=1\\) and the points \\((1,2),(1,3),\\dots ,(1,n-1)\\) on the\nvertical line \\(x=1\\).  Apart from the point \\((c,1)\\) (if it exists) these\n\\(2n-4\\) points lie on distinct vertical lines different from \\(x=c\\); each of\nthem would require a different sunny line.  Hence at least \\(2n-4\\) sunny\nlines are needed, contradicting the hypothesis that the total number of lines\nis \\(n\\).\n\nThus any admissible family must contain **at least two** non\u2011sunny lines.\nConsequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Existence for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\\) that for each integer \\(k\\) with\n\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines satisfying the two required conditions and containing exactly \\(k\\)\nsunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  Basis \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(S_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).\n\n* \\(k=0\\): take the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) together with the\n  line \\(x+y=0\\) (which is also non\u2011sunny).  All three points lie on the first\n  two lines, the third line is superfluous.\n\n* \\(k=1\\): keep \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) and add the sunny line \\(y=2x-1\\), which\n  passes through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  The point \\((1,2)\\) is on \\(x=1\\).\n\nThus the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2  Inductive step\n\nAssume the statement