_2\\}\\bigr)\\;\\cup\\;\n\\{L_2,M\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines (we removed the old horizontal line\n\\(y=1\\) and inserted the new line \\(M\\)).  The number of sunny lines in\n\\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\) equals\n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{k'}_{\\text{sunny lines of }\\mathcal F_{\\,n-1,k'}}\n\\;+\\;\n\\begin{cases}\n1 &\\text{if }\\lambda\\text{ is sunny},\\\\[2pt]\n0 &\\text{if }\\lambda\\text{ is non\u2011sunny}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThus by choosing \\(\\lambda\\) we can obtain either \\(k=k'\\) or\n\\(k=k'+1\\).  Starting from the basis \\(n=3\\) (where both possibilities\n\\(k=0,1\\) are realised) and applying the inductive step repeatedly, we can\nrealise **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved\n\n* any admissible family must contain at least two non\u2011sunny lines, whence\n  \\(k\\le n-2\\);\n* for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a family of \\(n\\) distinct\n  lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny\n  lines does exist (by the explicit inductive construction).\n\nTherefore the set of all possible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution."
