vertical line \\(x=i\\) lies on \\(L\\). Since there are \\(n\\) possible columns, the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that \\(L\\) can contain is at most \\(n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Lemma 2  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\). Then \\(\\ell_i\\in\\mathcal L\\) for every \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nV=\\{i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\mid \\ell_i\\in\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad v=|V|.\n\\]\n\nThe lines in \\(V\\) are vertical; the remaining \\(n-v\\) lines are non‑vertical.  \n\nConsider a column \\(i\\notin V\\). Its points in \\(T_n\\) are exactly the set  \n\n\\[\nC_i=\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains \\(n+1-i\\) points. Since \\(\\ell_i\\) is absent, these points must be covered by the non‑vertical lines. By Lemma 1 each non‑vertical line can contribute **at most one** point of \\(C_i\\). Therefore the number of non‑vertical lines, \\(n-v\\), must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nn-v\\;\\ge\\;|C_i|=n+1-i\\qquad\\forall\\,i\\notin V. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(M=\\{i\\notin V\\}\\); then \\(|M|=n-v\\). Summing (7) over all \\(i\\in M\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n(n-v)^2\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{i\\in M}(n+1-i). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed cardinality \\(m=n-v\\) the right‑hand side of (8) is maximised when the missing columns are the smallest ones, i.e. \\(M=\\{1,2,\\dots ,m\\}\\). In that case  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{m}(n+1-i)=m n-\\frac{m(m-1)}{2}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus (8) implies  \n\n\\[\nm^{2}\\;\\ge\\;m n-\\frac{m(m-1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nDividing by the positive integer \\(m\\) (if \\(m=0\\) we are done) yields  \n\n\\[\nm\\;\\ge\\;n-\\frac{m-1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{m+1}{2}\\;\\ge\\;n-m\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;m\\ge n-m\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;2m\\ge n.\n\\]\n\nBut we also have from (7) that for the smallest missing column \\(i=1\\),\n\n\\[\nm=n-v\\;\\ge\\;n+1-1=n,\n\\]\n\nso \\(m\\ge n\\). Since \\(m\\le n\\) (there are only \\(n\\) columns), we must have \\(m=n\\). This forces \\(v=0\\), contradicting the existence of any vertical line unless **all** columns are present, i.e. \\(v=n\\).\n\nHence \\(V=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and every vertical line \\(\\ell_i\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Consequence for sunny lines\n\nAll lines forced by Lemma 2 are vertical, i.e. parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis. A sunny line is defined as a line that is **not** parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\). Therefore none of the forced lines is sunny. Because the family already contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, there is no capacity to add any further line; consequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines in any admissible configuration satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk=0.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nThe set \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct vertical lines, covers every point of \\(T_n\\) by (6), and contains no sunny line. Hence \\(k=0\\) is indeed attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Conclusion.**  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the only non‑negative integer \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
