            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T_n\\) consists of the integer points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\); its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (one‑point‑per‑column)\n\nIf a line \\(L\\) is not vertical, then for each integer \\(i\\) the intersection\n\\(L\\cap\\{(i,b)\\mid b\\in\\mathbb R\\}\\) contains at most one point.  \nConsequently \\(L\\) contains at most one point of \\(T_n\\) from each column\n\\(x=i\\), and therefore at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) in total.\n\n*Proof.*  A non‑vertical line has equation \\(y=mx+c\\) with finite slope \\(m\\).\nFixing \\(x=i\\) yields the unique ordinate \\(y=mi+c\\); hence at most one point\nwith abscissa \\(i\\) lies on \\(L\\).  Since there are \\(n\\) possible columns,\n\\(|L\\cap T_n|\\le n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (necessity of a line through each point of column 1)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that every point of\n\\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nThen for each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) there is a line of\n\\(\\mathcal L\\) that passes through the point \\((1,i)\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Column 1 of the triangle consists of the \\(n\\) points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n).\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma 1 a single line can contain **at most one** of these points,\nbecause any two distinct points of column 1 have the same abscissa and a line\ncannot be vertical and non‑vertical simultaneously.  \nSince \\(\\mathcal L\\) has only \\(n\\) lines, covering the \\(n\\) points of column 1\nforces the existence of a distinct line for each of them. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction of families with a prescribed number of sunny lines\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=\\frac{1-i}{i}\\,x+1 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L_i\\) passes through \\((1,i)\\) (substituting \\(x=1\\) gives \\(y=i\\))\nand through \\((i+1,1)\\).  \nIts slope is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{1-i}{i}\\); for \\(i\\ge 1\\) this slope is\ndifferent from \\(0\\), from \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), and from \\(-1\\).  Hence every\n\\(L_i\\) is **sunny**.\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_n=\\{L_1,L_2,\\dots ,L_n\\}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines.  Because each \\(L_i\\) passes through the\npoint \\((1,i)\\), Lemma 2 guarantees that any family of \\(n\\) lines covering\n\\(T_n\\) must contain a line through each \\((1,i)\\); the lines in \\(\\mathcal\nS_n\\) fulfil exactly this requirement, so \\(\\mathcal S_n\\) indeed covers all\npoints of \\(T_n\\).  (A point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on \\(L_a\\), because substituting\n\\(x=a\\) into (1) yields \\(y=b\\) by the defining relation \\(a+b=n+1\\).)\n\nThus we have exhibited a configuration with **\\(k=n\\)** sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Reducing the number of sunny lines\n\nFor any subset \\(U\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) replace, for each \\(i\\in U\\), the\nsunny line \\(L_i\\) by the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\; x=i .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe vertical line \\(V_i\\) is not sunny (it is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) but\nstill passes through the required point \\((1,i)\\).  Consequently the new\nfamily  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_U=\\bigl\\{\\,L_i\\mid i\\notin U\\,\\bigr\\}\\cup\\bigl\\{\\,V_i\\mid i\\in U\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(|U|\\) non‑sunny lines and \\(n-|U|\\) sunny lines, i.e. it\ncontains \\(k=n-|U|\\) sunny lines.\n\nBecause each vertical line \\(V_i\\) contains **all** points of column \\(i\\),\nevery point of \\(T_n\\) that was previously covered by \\(L_i\\) remains covered,\nand points of other columns are unaffected.  Hence \\(\\mathcal L_U\\) still\ncovers the whole set \\(T_n\\).\n\nBy choosing \\(|U|=n-k\\) we obtain, for **any integer \\(k\\) with\n\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)**, a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of\n\\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Exhaustiveness\n\nThe problem asks for non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which such a family\nexists.  Since we have exhibited a construction for every \\(k\\) in the range\n\\(0\\le k\\le n\\), all these values are admissible.  No larger value of \\(k\\)\nis possible because there are only \\(n\\) lines in total.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
