e\npoint \\((1,i)\\).\n\n*Proof.*  The points \\((1,i)\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) are all distinct and belong to\ncolumn 1.  By Lemma 1 a single line can contain at most one of them, and we\nhave only \\(n\\) lines; therefore each of the \\(n\\) points needs its own line.\n∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### A covering family with all lines sunny  \n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=\\frac{1-i}{i}\\,x+1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Properties of \\(L_i\\).*\n\n* It passes through \\((1,i)\\) because substituting \\(x=1\\) gives \\(y=i\\).\n* It also passes through \\((i+1,1)\\) (substituting \\(x=i+1\\) yields \\(y=1\\)).\n* Its slope \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1-i}{i}\\) is different from \\(0\\), from\n  \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and from \\(-1\\); hence \\(L_i\\) is **sunny**.\n\n*Covering claim.*  \nWe claim that the family \\(\\mathcal S_n=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) covers every\npoint of \\(T_n\\).\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  Because \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we\nhave \\(b\\le n+1-a\\) and, in particular, \\(b\\le a\\) (otherwise \\(a+b>a+a\\ge\nn+2\\), contradicting the definition of \\(T_n\\)).  Hence \\(b\\) is an integer\nwith \\(1\\le b\\le a\\).  The line \\(L_a\\) contains exactly the points  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,1),\\;(a-1,2),\\dots ,(1,a)\\},\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nwhich is immediate from (5) by solving for \\(y\\):\n\\[\ny= \\frac{1-a}{a}\\,x+1 \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\na\\,y = (1-a)x + a \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\na\\,y + (a-1)x = a .\n\\]\nSetting \\(x=j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le a\\) gives \\(y=a+1-j\\); thus the points in\n(6) are precisely those with coordinates \\((j,a+1-j)\\).  Since \\(b\\le a\\) we\ncan write \\(b=a+1-(a+1-b)\\) and obtain \\((a,b)=(a+1-b,b)\\) with\n\\(1\\le a+1-b\\le a\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the list (6) and\nhence lies on \\(L_a\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is on the line \\(L_a\\) where \\(a\\) is its first\ncoordinate, and the family \\(\\mathcal S_n\\) indeed covers \\(T_n\\).  All its\n\\(n\\) lines are sunny, giving a configuration with \\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Replacing sunny lines by non‑sunny vertical lines  \n\nFor any \\(i\\) the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\; x=i\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nis not sunny (it is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) but it still passes through\nthe mandatory point \\((1,i)\\) of column 1.  Moreover, \\(V_i\\) contains **all**\npoints of column \\(i\\); therefore if we replace \\(L_i\\) by \\(V_i\\) we do not\nlose any point that was previously covered, and we may even cover additional\npoints.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Obtaining any prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  Choose a subset\n\\(U\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) of size \\(|U|=n-k\\).  Define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_U=\n\\bigl\\{\\,L_i\\mid i\\notin U\\,\\bigr\\}\\cup\n\\bigl\\{\\,V_i\\mid i\\in U\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_U\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines:\nthe \\(k\\) lines \\(L_i\\) with \\(i\\notin U\\) are sunny, and the \\(n-k\\) lines\n\\(V_i\\) with \\(i\\in U\\) are not sunny.  By the discussion of step 5,\nreplacing each \\(L_i\\) by \\(V_i\\) (for \\(i\\in U\\)) does not destroy the\ncovering property; consequently \\(\\mathcal L_U\\) still covers every point of\n\\(T_n\\).  Hence we have produced a configuration with precisely \\(k\\) sunny\nlines.\n\nSince \\(k\\) was arbitrary in \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), all these values are\nrealizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Exhaustiveness  \n\nA configuration contains exactly \\(n\\) lines, so it cannot have more than\n\\(n\\) sunny lines.  The construction above shows that every integer between\n\\(0\\) and \\(n\\) is attainable.  Therefore the set of admissible values of\n\\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n } .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
