a=t\\) this gives \\(b = t + (n-t)=n\\); thus the only point of the vertical column \\(x=t\\) that lies on \\(S_t\\) is \\((t,n)\\). Consequently the points \\((t,b)\\) with \\(b<n\\) are not on \\(S_t\\).  \n\n    Nevertheless they are covered by the vertical line \\(L_{b}\\) because \\(b\\ge 1\\) and \\(b\\neq t\\) (otherwise we would be in the previous sub\u2011case). Indeed, the point \\((t,b)\\) has abscissa \\(t\\) and ordinate \\(b\\); swapping the roles, the line \\(L_{b}\\) is \\(x=b\\) and contains \\((t,b)\\) exactly when \\(t=b\\), which is false. Hence we need a different argument.\n\n    Actually, for any \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a\\neq t\\) the original covering already guarantees a line containing it (either \\(L_a\\) or \\(L_n\\)). The only points that could become uncovered are those with abscissa \\(t\\) and ordinate \\(b<n\\). Such points satisfy \\(t+b\\le n\\). Because \\(b\\ge 1\\) we have \\(b\\le n-1\\) and consequently \\(b\\le n-t\\). Hence  \n\n    \\[\n    b = (n-t) - (n-t-b) = (n-t) - c\n    \\]\n\n    for some non\u2011negative integer \\(c\\). Then the point \\((a,b)=(t,\\,n-t-c)\\) lies on the sunny line  \n\n    \\[\n    S_{t-c}:\\; y = x + (n-(t-c)) = x + (n-t+c),\n    \\]\n\n    because substituting \\(x=t\\) gives \\(y = t + (n-t+c)=n+c\\), which is not equal to \\(n-t-c\\). This shows the previous attempt fails.\n\n    **We therefore need a different construction** that guarantees that after replacing a vertical line by a sunny line, every point of the column \\(x=t\\) with \\(b<n\\) remains covered by some other line that has not been altered.  \n\n    The simplest way is to replace *pairs* of vertical lines simultaneously by two sunny lines that together still cover the whole triangular set.  \n\n    For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\}\\) replace the two vertical lines \\(L_i\\) and \\(L_{n-i}\\) by the sunny lines  \n\n    \\[\n    S_i:\\; y = x + (n-i),\\qquad\n    S_{n-i}:\\; y = -x + (n+i