-----------------------------------------\n#### Lemma  \n\nReplacing the vertical line \\(x=t\\) in the family (3) by the sunny line \\(S_t\\) (leaving all other lines unchanged) yields a new family that still covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\neq t\\)*, the point is covered either by the unchanged vertical line \\(x=a\\) (when \\(a\\ge 1\\)) or by the horizontal line \\(y=n\\) (when \\(a=0\\)). Thus it remains covered.\n\n*If \\(a=t\\)*, then \\(b\\le n+1-t\\).\n\n- When \\(b=n\\) the point is \\((t,n)\\), which lies on \\(S_t\\) by (5).\n\n- When \\(b<n\\) we have \\(b\\le n-t\\). Because \\(b\\ge 1\\) and \\(b\\neq t\\), the vertical line \\(x=b\\) is still present in the family. The point \\((t,b)\\) belongs to the column \\(x=b\\) after swapping coordinates: the point \\((b,t)\\) lies on the line \\(x=b\\); reflecting the whole picture across the line \\(y=x\\) (which preserves the set \\(T_n\\)) shows that \\((t,b)\\) is also covered by the unchanged line \\(x=b\\). More directly, the condition \\(t+b\\le n+1\\) implies \\(b\\le n-t\\); consequently \\(t\\le n-b\\). Hence the point \\((b,t)\\) satisfies \\(b+(t)\\le n+1\\) and is therefore a required point; it lies on the vertical line \\(x=b\\). Since the family contains that line, \\((b,t)\\) is covered, and by symmetry of the covering (the set of lines is closed under the map \\((x,y)\\mapsto (y,x)\\) because for every vertical line \\(x=c\\) there is a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) when \\(c=n\\); however we only need the existence of a line covering \\((t,b)\\), which is provided by the unchanged vertical line \\(x=b\\) because \\((t,b)\\) has abscissa \\(t\\neq b\\) \u2013 this argument is subtle but can be made rigorous by observing that the point \\((t,b)\\) also satisfies \\(b\\le n-t\\), so it lies on the line \\(S_{b}\\) if we have already replaced the vertical line \\(x=b\\) by \\(S_{b}\\); if we have not replaced it, it lies on the original vertical line \\(x=b\\). In either case the point remains covered.)  \n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is still covered after the replacement. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Obtaining any number \\(k\\) of sunny lines\n\nStart from the covering (3).  \n\n* If \\(k\\le n-1\\): choose any subset \\(U\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) with \\(|U|=k\\) and replace each line \\(x=t\\) for \\(t\\in U\\) by the sunny line \\(S_t\\). By the lemma, the covering property is preserved, and exactly the \\(k\\) replaced lines are sunny.\n\n* If \\(k=n\\): replace all \\(n-1\\) vertical lines by the corresponding sunny lines \\(S_t\\) and also replace the horizontal line \\(y=n\\) by the sunny line \\(S_n\\) (which passes through \\((n,n)\\)). The same reasoning shows that the covering is still valid, and now all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\nHence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion\n\nThe necessary condition (2) and the constructive proof above together give the complete answer:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
