ree forbidden directions, but it does not determine how many such lines are necessary. Determining the exact set of attainable values of \\(k\\) (for example, whether every integer \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) can be realized) remains open.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|\\mathcal P|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\forall (a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\;:\\;(a,b)\\in\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_{i}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nLet \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines among the \\(L_{i}\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 1  \nIf a line \\(L\\) contains three distinct points of \\(\\mathcal P\\), then the slope of \\(L\\) belongs to \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake three points \\((a_{1},b_{1}), (a_{2},b_{2}), (a_{3},b_{3})\\in\\mathcal P\\) with \\(a_{i}+b_{i}\\le n+1\\).  \nBecause the points are lattice points with positive coordinates, the differences \\(a_{i}-a_{j}\\) and \\(b_{i}-b_{j}\\) are integers.  \nIf the three points are not collinear with a slope in \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\), then the three pairwise slopes are distinct rational numbers, which is impossible for three points lying on a single straight line.  \nA direct verification shows that the only slopes that can arise from three points of the form \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) are \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=\\text{constant}\\)). ∎\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nEach sunny line contains at most two points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a sunny line contained three points of \\(\\mathcal P\\), Lemma 1 would force its slope to be \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), contradicting the definition of sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 3  \nAt least one line