\) is sunny. Moreover \\(S_{t}\\) passes through \\((t,n)\\) because \\(n = t + (n-t)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \n\nLet \\(\\mathcal V\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nForm the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)=\\bigl(\\{x=i\\mid i\\notin\\mathcal V\\}\\bigr)\n                     \\;\\cup\\;\n                     \\bigl(\\{S_{t}\\mid t\\in\\mathcal V\\}\\bigr)\n                     \\;\\cup\\;\\{y=n\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThen \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\) covers \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\notin\\mathcal V\\)*, the vertical line \\(x=a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\) and contains the point.\n\n*Assume \\(a\\in\\mathcal V\\).*  \nBecause \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).\n\n*Subcase 1:* \\(b=n\\).  \nThen \\((a,b)=(a,n)\\) lies on \\(S_{a}\\) by (7).\n\n*Subcase 2:* \\(b<n\\).  \nDefine \\(c=n-a-b\\). From \\(b\\le n+1-a\\) we obtain \\(c\\ge 0\\) and \\(c\\) is an integer.  \nConsider the line \\(S_{a+c}\\). Since \\(a\\le n-1\\) and \\(c\\ge 0\\), we have \\(a+c\\le n\\); consequently \\(S_{a+c}\\) is present in \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\) (it is either a sunny line, if \\(a+c\\in\\mathcal V\\), or the original vertical line \\(x=a+c\\), if \\(a+c\\notin\\mathcal V\\)).  \n\nSubstituting \\(x=a\\) into the equation of \\(S_{a+c}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = a + \\bigl(n-(a+c)\\bigr)= n-c .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(c=n-a-b\\), the right‑hand side equals \\(b\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(S_{a+c}\\), which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\).\n\nAll possibilities have been covered, so every point of \\(T_{n}\\) is contained in some line of \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\n*Case \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).*  \nChoose any subset \\(\\mathcal V\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) with \\(|\\mathcal V|=k\\).  \nThe family \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\) defined in (8) contains exactly the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\{S_{t}\\mid t\\in\\mathcal V\\}\\) and, by Lemma 1, covers \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n*Case \\(k=n\\).*  \nTake \\(\\mathcal V=\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) (all vertical lines are replaced) and replace the remaining horizontal line \\(y=n\\) by the sunny line \\(S_{n}\\).  \nThe resulting family is \\(\\{S_{1},S_{2},\\dots ,S_{n}\\}\\); it consists of \\(n\\) sunny lines.  \nLemma 1 does not use the horizontal line, so the covering property still holds.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have produced \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Final answer\n\nCombining the necessary condition (5) with the constructions above, the set of all admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
