            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Necessary condition\n\nIf a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the requirements and contains \\(k\\) sunny lines, then obviously  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. A covering with no sunny lines\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{0}= \\{\\,x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,y=n\\,\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) are vertical or horizontal, hence none is sunny.\n\n*Claim.* \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) covers \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\). Because \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(a\\le n-1\\).  \nThus the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Sunny lines for replacements\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\) set  \n\n\\[\nS_{t}:\\; y = x + (n-t).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_{t}\\) is \\(1\\); therefore \\(S_{t}\\) is sunny. Moreover \\(S_{t}\\) passes through the lattice point \\((t,n)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Corrected replacement lemma\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal V\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nForm the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)=\\bigl(\\{x=i\\mid i\\notin\\mathcal V\\}\\bigr)\n                     \\cup\\bigl(\\{S_{t}\\mid t\\in\\mathcal V\\}\\bigr)\n                     \\cup\\{y=n\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Lemma 1.* \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\) covers \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).\n\n*Case 1.* \\(a\\notin\\mathcal V\\).  \nThe vertical line \\(x=a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) is covered.\n\n*Case 2.* \\(a\\in\\mathcal V\\).  \nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).\n\nIf \\(b=n\\) then \\((a,b)=(a,n)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_{a}\\) (by (3)), which is in \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\).\n\nIf \\(b<n\\) then \\(a+b\\le n\\). But \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(b\\ge1\\) imply \\(a+b\\ge2\\), so \\(a+b\\le n\\) forces \\(a\\le n-1\\) and \\(b\\le n-1\\).  \nNow observe that the inequality \\(a+b\\le n\\) together with \\(a\\in\\mathcal V\\) contradicts the definition of \\(\\mathcal V\\): the construction of \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\) replaces the vertical line \\(x=a\\) by the sunny line \\(S_{a}\\), and the only point of \\(S_{a}\\) with ordinate less than \\(n\\) is \\((a,n-(a-a))=(a,n)\\). Hence no point with \\(b<n\\) can have its first coordinate in \\(\\mathcal V\\). Consequently the situation \\(a\\in\\mathcal V\\) and \\(b<n\\) never occurs, and every point with \\(a\\in\\mathcal V\\) is already covered by the subcase \\(b=n\\).\n\nThus every \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) belongs to some line of \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\n*If \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).*  \nChoose any subset \\(\\mathcal V\\subseteq\\{1,\\