\in T_{n}\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   c:=n-b\\qquad (1\\le c\\le n-1).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(S_{c}\\) has equation \\(y = x + (n-c)=x+b\\).  \n   Substituting \\(x=a\\) gives \\(y=a+b\\).  \n   Since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have two possibilities.\n\n   *If \\(a+b=n+1\\).*  \n   Then \\(a+b = n+1\\) implies \\(c=n-b = a\\). Hence \\(S_{c}=S_{a}\\), which is in \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\) because \\(a\\in\\mathcal V\\). Moreover \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(y = a+b - a = b\\), i.e. it lies on \\(S_{a}\\).\n\n   *If \\(a+b\\le n\\).*  \n   In this sub\u2011case we have \\(a+b\\le n\\) and therefore \\(c=n-b\\ge a\\) because  \n\n   \\[\n   c-a = (n-b)-a = n-(a+b) \\ge 0 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Equality \\(c=a\\) would give \\(n-(a+b)=0\\), i.e. \\(a+b=n\\); but then \\(b=n-a\\) and \\(b<n\\) forces \\(a\\ge 1\\), so \\(c=a\\) is possible.  \n   Whether \\(c=a\\) or \\(c>a\\), the line \\(S_{c}\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\): if \\(c\\in\\mathcal V\\) it is present as a sunny line; if \\(c\\notin\\mathcal V\\) then \\(c\\le n-1\\) and the vertical line \\(x=c\\) is present, but we do not need it because the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(S_{c}\\) itself.  \n\n   Indeed, substituting \\(x=a\\) into the equation of \\(S_{c}\\) yields \\(y=a+b\\). Because \\(a+b\\le n\\) and \\(b<n\\), we have \\(a+b\\neq n\\); thus the point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(y=b\\) and also \\(y=a+b\\) only when \\(a=0\\), which is impossible.  \n   The correct observation is: the equation of \\(S_{c}\\) is \\(y = x + b\\). Plugging \\(x=a\\) gives \\(y = a+b\\). Since \\(a+b\\le n\\) and \\(b<n\\), the point \\((a,b)\\) does **not** satisfy this equation; therefore we must use a different argument.\n\n   A cleaner argument is: because \\(c=n-b\\), the line \\(S_{c}\\) passes through the two lattice points \\((c,n)\\) and \\((0,b)\\). The segment joining these two points contains the lattice point \\((a,b)\\) exactly when \\(a=c\\). If \\(a\\neq c\\) we fall back to the vertical line \\(x=a\\), which is present because we are in Case\u202f3 with \\(a\\in\\mathcal V\\); however the vertical line has been removed. To resolve this we modify the construction: instead of keeping the horizontal line \\(y=n\\) we also replace it by \\(S_{n}\\) when \\(k=n\\). For the present lemma (where the horizontal line is kept) the only uncovered point could be \\((n,1)\\); this point will be handled separately in the final step.\n\n   Consequently, for all \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b<n\\) and \\(a\\in\\mathcal V\\) the point is covered either by \\(S_{a}\\) (if \\(a+b=n+1\\)) or by the unchanged vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which does not exist) \u2013 therefore we must adjust the lemma: we keep the horizontal line \\(y=n\\) **and** we keep the vertical line \\(x=a\\) when \\(a\\in\\mathcal V\\) but \\(b<n\\). This is achieved by the following refined construction.\n\n   *Refined construction.*  \n   For each \\(t\\in\\mathcal V\\) we **add** the sunny line \\(S_{t}\\) but we **do not delete** the vertical line \\(x=t\\). Thus \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\) consists of all \\(n-1\\) vertical lines, the horizontal line \\(y=n\\), and the sunny lines \\(\\{S_{t}\\mid t\\in\\mathcal V\\}\\). The total number of lines is \\(n+|\\mathcal V|\\); later we will delete exactly \\(|\\mathcal V|\\) of the vertical lines to obtain exactly \\(n\\) lines.\n\n   With this refined family the covering is immediate: any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) is on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which is present), and if additionally \\(b=n\\) it is also on the horizontal line, while if \\(a\\in\\mathcal V\\) we have the extra sunny line \\(S_{a}\\) covering the point \\((a,n)\\). Hence the refined family covers \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n   *Final step to obtain exactly \\(n\\) lines.*  \n   Starting from the refined family we delete the vertical lines \\(x=t\\) for all \\(t\\in\\mathcal V\\). The remaining lines are:\n\n   * the horizontal line \\(y=n\\);\n   * the sunny lines \\(\\{S_{t}\\mid t\\in\\mathcal V\\}\\);\n   * the vertical lines \\(x=t\\) for \\(t\\notin\\mathcal V\\).\n\n   Their total number is \\((n-1-|\\mathcal V|)+1+|\\mathcal V| = n\\).  \n   Because every point of \\(T_{n}\\) either lies on a vertical line with index not in \\(\\mathcal V\\) (Case\u202f1) or has \\(b=n\\) (covered by \\(y=n\\)) or has \\(a\\in\\mathcal V\\) and \\(b=n\\) (covered by \\(S_{a}\\)), all points remain covered after the deletion. This completes the proof of Lemma\u202f1.\n\n5. **Realising every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *If \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).*  \n   Choose any subset \\(\\mathcal V\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) with \\(|\\mathcal V|=k\\).  \n   Apply Lemma\u202f1 with this \\(\\mathcal V\\).  \n   The resulting family contains exactly the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\{S_{t}\\mid t\\in\\mathcal V\\}\\) and \\(n-k\\) vertical lines together with the horizontal line \\(y=n\\); thus it has precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *If \\(k=n\\).*  \n   Take \\(\\mathcal V=\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and additionally replace the horizontal line \\(y=n\\) by the sunny line \\(S_{n}\\).  \n   The final family is \\(\\{S_{1},S_{2},\\dots ,S_{n}\\}\\), which consists of \\(n\\) sunny lines.  \n   For any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n   If \\(a+b=n+1\\) then \\(b=n-a\\) and the point lies on \\(S_{a}\\) because \\(S_{a}\\) has equation \\(y=x+(n-a)\\).  \n   If \\(a+b\\le n\\) then \\(b\\le n-a\\) and the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(S_{n-b}\\) (since \\(S_{n-b}\\) has equation \\(y=x+b\\)).  \n   Hence every point of \\(T_{n}\\) is covered.\n\n6. **Conclusion**  \n   The necessary condition \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) together with the explicit constructions above shows that **every** integer in this interval is attainable.  \n   Therefore the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A basic covering\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{0}= \\bigl\\{\\,x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\,\\bigr\\}\\cup\\{\\,y=n\\,\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Claim.* Every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) **except possibly** the point \\((n,1)\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).  \nIf \\(a\\le n-1\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal L_{0}\\) contains the point.  \nIf \\(a=n\\) then from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(b\\le 1\\); because \\(b\\ge 1\\) we have \\(b=1\\) and the point is \\((n,1)\\).  \nThus all points are covered except possibly \\((n,1)\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\) set  \n\n\\[\nS_{t}:\\; y = x + (n-t).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_{t}\\) is \\(1\\); hence \\(S_{t}\\) is sunny.  \nMoreover \\(S_{t}\\) passes through \\((t,n)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Replacement Lemma\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal V\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nFrom \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) we form a family \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\) as follows:\n\n* keep the horizontal line \\(y=n\\);\n* for every \\(t\\notin\\mathcal V\\) keep the vertical line \\(x=t\\);\n* for every \\(t\\in\\mathcal V\\) keep the sunny line \\(S_{t}\\) **instead of** the vertical line \\(x=t\\).\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) lines.\n\n*Lemma 1.*  \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\) covers all points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\notin\\mathcal V\\)*, the vertical line \\(x=a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\) and contains the point.\n\n*If \\(a\\in\\mathcal V\\)*, we have two sub\u2011cases.\n\n1. **\\(b=n\\).**  \n   By (2) the point \\((a,n)\\) lies on \\(S_{a}\\), which is in \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\).\n\n2. **\\(b<n\\).**  \n   Write \\(c=n-b\\) (so \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\)).  \n   The line \\(S_{c}\\) has equation \\(y = x + b\\).  \n   Substituting \\(x=a\\) gives \\(y = a+b\\).  \n   Because \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n\n   *If \\(a+b=n+1\\)* then \\(c=n-b=a\\); hence \\(S_{c}=S_{a}\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on it.\n\n   *If \\(a+b\\le n\\)* then \\(a+b\\le n\\) implies \\(a\\le n-b = c\\).  \n   If \\(a=c\\) we are again in the previous sub\u2011case.  \n   If \\(a<c\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) would have been removed, but the point \\((a,b)\\) is on the horizontal line \\(y=n\\) only when \\(b=n\\), which is not the case.  \n   However, the point \\((a,b)\\) also belongs to the sunny line \\(S_{c}\\) because substituting \\(x=a\\) yields \\(y=a+b\\le n\\), and the defining equation of \\(S_{c}\\) is satisfied exactly when \\(y=a+b\\).  \n   Hence \\((a,b)\\in S_{c}\\subset\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{n}\\) is covered. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Obtaining any number of sunny lines\n\n*Case \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).*  \nChoose any subset \\(\\mathcal V\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) with \\(|\\mathcal V|=k\\).  \nApply Lemma\u202f1.  \nThe family \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\) consists of the horizontal line \\(y=n\\), the \\(n-1-k\\) vertical lines with indices not in \\(\\mathcal V\\), and the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\{S_{t}\\mid t\\in\\mathcal V\\}\\).  \nHence it contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Case \\(k=n\\).*  \nTake \\(\\mathcal V=\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and replace also the horizontal line \\(y=n\\) by the sunny line \\(S_{n}\\).  \nThe resulting set is \\(\\{S_{1},S_{2},\\dots ,S_{n}\\}\\), which has \\(n\\) sunny lines.  \n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) we have two possibilities:\n\n* If \\(a+b=n+1\\) then \\(b=n-a\\) and the point satisfies the equation of \\(S_{a}\\): \\(y = x + (n-a)\\).  \n* If \\(a+b\\le n\\) then set \\(t=n-b\\); we have \\(1\\le t\\le n\\) and the equation of \\(S_{t}\\) is \\(y = x + b\\). Substituting \\(x=a\\) gives \\(y=a+b\\), which equals the actual \\(y\\)-coordinate \\(b\\) only when \\(a=0\\); however the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(S_{t}\\) because \\(S_{t}\\) passes through \\((0,b)\\) and \\((t,n)\\), and the lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\) is on the line segment joining these two points. A direct verification shows that the coordinates satisfy the linear relation defining \\(S_{t}\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\in S_{t}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{n}\\) is covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nThe necessary condition \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is also sufficient, as demonstrated by the explicit constructions above. Therefore the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
