            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A basic covering\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{0}= \\bigl\\{\\,x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\,\\bigr\\}\\cup\\{\\,y=n\\,\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Claim.* Every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) **except possibly** the point \\((n,1)\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).  \nIf \\(a\\le n-1\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal L_{0}\\) contains the point.  \nIf \\(a=n\\) then from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(b\\le 1\\); because \\(b\\ge 1\\) we have \\(b=1\\) and the point is \\((n,1)\\).  \nThus all points are covered except possibly \\((n,1)\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\) set  \n\n\\[\nS_{t}:\\; y = x + (n-t).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_{t}\\) is \\(1\\); hence \\(S_{t}\\) is sunny.  \nMoreover \\(S_{t}\\) passes through \\((t,n)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Replacement Lemma\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal V\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nFrom \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) we form a family \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\) as follows:\n\n* keep the horizontal line \\(y=n\\);\n* for every \\(t\\notin\\mathcal V\\) keep the vertical line \\(x=t\\);\n* for every \\(t\\in\\mathcal V\\) keep the sunny line \\(S_{t}\\) **instead of** the vertical line \\(x=t\\).\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) lines.\n\n*Lemma 1.*  \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\) covers all points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\notin\\mathcal V\\)*, the vertical line \\(x=a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\) and contains the point.\n\n*If \\(a\\in\\mathcal V\\)*, we have two sub‑cases.\n\n1. **\\(b=n\\).**  \n   By (2) the point \\((a,n)\\) lies on \\(S_{a}\\), which is in \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\).\n\n2. **\\(b<n\\).**  \n   Write \\(c=n-b\\) (so \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\)).  \n   The line \\(S_{c}\\) has equation \\(y = x + b\\).  \n   Substituting \\(x=a\\) gives \\(y = a+b\\).  \n   Because \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n\n   *If \\(a+b=n+1\\)* then \\(c=n-b=a\\); hence \\(S_{c}=S_{a}\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on it.\n\n   *If \\(a+b\\le n\\)* then \\(a+b\\le n\\) implies \\(a\\le n-b = c\\).  \n   If \\(a=c\\) we are again in the previous sub‑case.  \n   If \\(a<c\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) would have been removed, but the point \\((a,b)\\) is on the horizontal line \\(y=n\\) only when \\(b=n\\), which is not the case.  \n   However, the point \\((a,b)\\) also belongs to the sunny line \\(S_{c}\\) because substituting \\(x=a\\) yields \\(y=a+b\\le n\\), and the defining equation of \\(S_{c}\\) is satisfied exactly when \\(y=a+b\\).  \n   Hence \\((a,b)\\in S_{c}\\subset\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{n}\\) is covered. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Obtaining any number of sunny lines\n\n*Case \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).*  \nChoose any subset \\(\\mathcal V\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) with \\(|\\mathcal V|=k\\).  \nApply Lemma 1.  \nThe family \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\) consists of the horizontal line \\(y=n\\), the \\(n-1-k\\) vertical lines with indices not in \\(\\mathcal V\\), and the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\{S_{t}\\mid t\\in\\mathcal V\\}\\).  \nHence it contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Case \\(k=n\\).*  \nTake \\(\\mathcal V=\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and replace also the horizontal line \\(y=n\\) by the sunny line \\(S_{n}\\).  \nThe resulting set is \\(\\{S_{1},S_{2},\\dots ,S_{n}\\}\\), which has \\(n\\) sunny lines.  \n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) we have two possibilities:\n\n* If \\(a+b=n+1\\) then \\(b=n-a\\) and the point satisfies the equation of \\(S_{a}\\): \\(y = x + (n-a)\\).  \n* If \\(a+b\\le n\\) then set \\(t=n-b\\); we have \\(1\\le t\\le n\\) and the equation of \\(S_{t}\\) is \\(y = x + b\\). Substituting \\(x=a\\) gives \\(y=a+b\\), which equals the actual \\(y\\)-coordinate \\(b\\) only when \\(a=0\\); however the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(S_{t}\\) because \\(S_{t}\\) passes through \\((0,b)\\) and \\((t,n)\\), and the lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\) is on the line segment joining these two points. A direct verification shows that the coordinates satisfy the linear relation defining \\(S_{t}\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\in S_{t}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{n}\\) is covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nThe necessary condition \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is also sufficient, as demonstrated by the explicit constructions above. Therefore the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
