) contains every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(0\\le a\\le c\\) and the same ordinate \\(b\\); in particular \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line determined by these two points, i.e. on \\(S_{c}\\).  \n     Algebraically, the collinearity condition for \\((0,b),(a,b),(c,n)\\) is  \n\n     \\[\n     \\frac{b-b}{a-0}= \\frac{n-b}{c-0}\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad 0 = \\frac{n-b}{c},\n     \\]\n\n     which holds because the left‑hand side is zero and the right‑hand side is also zero (the numerator \\(n-b\\) equals \\(c\\) by the definition \\(c=n-b\\)).  \n     Hence \\((a,b)\\) indeed lies on \\(S_{c}\\).\n\n     Thus in every sub‑case the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to a line of \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\).\n\n   All possibilities have been exhausted, so every point of \\(T_{n}\\) is covered. ∎\n\n5. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\).**  \n   Suppose we had \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_{n}\\).  \n   Any sunny line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   Consider the two extreme points of \\(T_{n}\\): \\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\).  \n   Because the slope \\(-1\\) is forbidden, no single line can contain both points.  \n   Hence at least two lines are needed for these two points.  \n   Removing any line leaves at most \\(n-1\\) lines, which is insufficient to cover the remaining \\(n-2\\) points on the “border” \\(a+b=n+1\\) (there are exactly \\(n\\) such points).  \n   A more formal counting argument: each sunny line can intersect the set \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\\) in at most one point, because two distinct points of that set determine a line of slope \\(-1\\), which is prohibited.  \n   Therefore covering the \\(n\\) points of the diagonal \\(a+b=n+1\\) requires at least \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \n   If all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, each of them would have to contain exactly one of those diagonal points, leaving no line available to cover any interior point (those with \\(a+b\\le n\\)).  \n   Consequently the interior points cannot all be covered, a contradiction.  \n   Hence \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n6. **Realising every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   For any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) choose an arbitrary subset \\(\\mathcal V\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) of size \\(k\\) and apply Lemma 1.  \n   The resulting family \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\) consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines and covers all points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n   Therefore every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is attainable, and no larger \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nThe set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
