at most one** point.  \n   Because \\(|D_{n}|=n\\), covering all points of \\(D_{n}\\) with sunny lines would require at least \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines.  \n   If all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, each of them would contain exactly one point of \\(D_{n}\\) and none could contain any interior point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\) (otherwise that line would contain two points of \\(D_{n}\\), contradicting Lemma 2).  \n   Hence the interior points could not be covered – a contradiction.  \n   Therefore **\\(k=n\\) is impossible**, and any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le n-1 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n3. **A basic covering with no sunny lines.**  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{0}= \\bigl\\{\\,x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\,\\bigr\\}\\cup\\{\\,y=n\\,\\}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   All lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) are vertical or horizontal, hence non‑sunny.  \n   For any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) we have two possibilities:\n\n   *If \\(a\\le n-1\\)*, the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal L_{0}\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).  \n   *If \\(a=n\\)*, then \\(b\\le 1\\) by (1), so \\((n,1)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=n\\in\\mathcal L_{0}\\).\n\n   Thus \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) covers every point of \\(T_{n}\\) and contains exactly \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n4. **Sunny replacement lines.**  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{t}:\\; y = x + (n-t).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Its slope is \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(S_{t}\\) is sunny.  \n   Moreover \\(S_{t}\\) passes through the lattice point \\((t,n)\\).\n\n5. **Replacement Lemma (rigorous).**  \n\n   *Lemma 3.*  \n   Let \\(\\mathcal V\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) with \\(|\\mathcal V|=k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)).  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)=\n   \\bigl\\{\\,x=i\\mid i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\setminus\\mathcal V\\,\\bigr\\}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\bigl\\{\\,S_{t}\\mid t\\in\\mathcal V\\,\\bigr\\}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\\{\\,y=n\\,\\}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Then \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and it covers every point of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   The number of lines is \\((n-1-k)+k+1=n\\); the sunny lines are precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_{t}\\).\n\n   Take an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).\n\n   **Case 1.** \\(a\\notin\\mathcal V\\).  \n   Then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   **Case 2.** \\(a\\in\\mathcal V\\).  \n   *Subcase 2.1.* \\(b=n\\).  \n   The point \\((a,n)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_{a}\\) because substituting \\(x=a\\) in (5) gives \\(y=a+(n-a)=n\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) is covered.\n\n   *Subcase 2.2.* \\(b<n\\).  \n   Set \\(c:=n-b\\) (so \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\)).  \n   Consider the sunny line \\(S_{c}\\) given by \\(y = x + (n-c)=x+b\\).  \n   Substituting \\(x=a\\) yields \\(y=a+b\\).  \n   Since \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n   - If \\(a+b=n+1\\) then \\(c=n-b=a\\). Hence \\(S_{c}=S_{a}\\) and (5) gives \\(y=n\\) when \\(x=a\\); but because \\(b=n+1-a\\) we have \\(b=n\\), contradicting the hypothesis \\(b<n\\). Therefore this sub‑case cannot occur.\n\n   - If \\(a+b\\le n\\) then \\(a\\le n-b=c\\).  \n     The line \\(S_{c}\\) passes through the two lattice points \\((0,b)\\) and \\((c,n)\\).  \n     The equation of the line through these two points is exactly \\(y = x + b\\), i.e. \\(S_{c}\\).  \n     Because \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(y=b\\) and \\(x=a\\) with \\(0\\le a\\le c\\), the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the **segment** joining \\((0,b)\\) and \\((c,b)\\); however the line \\(S_{c}\\) is not horizontal, so we must verify collinearity directly.\n\n     The three points \\((0,b),\\;(a,b),\\;(c,n)\\) are collinear iff the determinant\n\n     \\[\n     \\begin{vmatrix}\n     0 & b & 1\\\\\n     a & b & 1\\\\\n     c & n & 1\n     \\end{vmatrix}=0 .\n     \\]\n\n     Computing,\n\n     \\[\n     0\\cdot(b-n)+a\\cdot(n-b)+c\\cdot(b-b)=a(n-b)=a\\,c .\n     \\]\n\n     Since \\(c=n-b\\), the determinant equals \\(a\\,c-a\\,c=0\\).  \n     Hence the three points are collinear, i.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line through \\((0,b)\\) and \\((c,n)\\), which is precisely \\(S_{c}\\).  \n\n     Therefore \\((a,b)\\in S_{c}\\subseteq\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\).\n\n   All possibilities have been exhausted, so every \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) belongs to a line of \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\). ∎\n\n6. **Realising every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   *For \\(k=0\\)* we simply take \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) from (4).  \n   *For \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\)* choose any subset \\(\\mathcal V\\subseteq\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) with \\(|\\mathcal V|=k\\) and use the family \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\) given by Lemma 3.  \n   *For \\(k=n-1\\)* we take \\(\\mathcal V=\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); then \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\) consists of the \\(n-2\\) sunny lines \\(S_{2},\\dots ,S_{n-1}\\), the vertical line \\(x=1\\), and the horizontal line \\(y=n\\). This family has exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines and, by Lemma 3, covers all points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n   Hence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the impossibility of \\(k=n\\) (Step 2) with the explicit constructions for all \\(k\\le n-1\\) (Steps 5–6), we obtain that the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-1\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions ab