)+k+1+1=n\\) lines. The sunny lines are precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_{t}\\).\n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\). We distinguish two cases.\n\n   **Case 1.** \\(a\\notin\\mathcal V\\).  \n   Then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   **Case 2.** \\(a\\in\\mathcal V\\).  \n   Write \\(c:=n-b\\) (so \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) because \\(b\\ge1\\)).  \n\n   *Subcase 2.1.* \\(b=n\\).  \n   Substituting \\(x=a\\) in (5) gives \\(y=a+(n-a)=n\\); hence \\((a,n)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_{a}\\in\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\).\n\n   *Subcase 2.2.* \\(b<n\\).  \n   Consider the sunny line \\(S_{c}\\) defined by (5). Its equation is \\(y = x + (n-c)=x+b\\).  \n   Substituting \\(x=a\\) yields \\(y=a+b\\). Because \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n   - If \\(a+b=n+1\\) then \\(c=n-b=a\\). In this situation \\(S_{c}=S_{a}\\) and, as in Subcase 2.1, the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on that line (indeed \\(b=n+1-a\\) gives \\(y=n\\) when \\(x=a\\), which is impossible because we are in the sub‑case \\(b<n\\); therefore this equality cannot occur). Hence Subcase 2.2 never meets the boundary \\(a+b=n+1\\).\n\n   - Consequently we must have \\(a+b\\le n\\). Then \\(a\\le n-b=c\\). The three points \\((0,b),\\;(a,b),\\;(c,n)\\) are collinear because the determinant  \n\n     \\[\n     \\begin{vmatrix}\n     0 & b & 1\\\\\n     a & b & 1\\\\\n     c & n & 1\n     \\end{vmatrix}\n     =0\\cdot(b-n)+a(n-b)+c(b-b)=a(n-b)=a\\,c\n     \\]\n\n     equals zero (using \\(c=n-b\\)). The line through \\((0,b)\\) and \\((c,n)\\) has slope \\(\\dfrac{n-b}{c}=1\\) and passes through \\((a,b)\\); its equation is exactly \\(y = x + b\\), i.e. the line \\(S_{c}\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\in S_{c}\\subseteq\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\).\n\n   All possibilities are covered, so every point of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs to a line of \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\). ∎\n\n6. **Realising every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Take \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) from (4).  \n\n   *\\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).*  Choose any subset \\(\\mathcal V\\subseteq\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) with \\(|\\mathcal V|=k\\) and use the family \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\) given by Lemma 2.  \n\n   *\\(k=n-1\\).*  Let \\(\\mathcal V=\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) (so \\(|\\mathcal V|=n-2\\)).  \n   Then \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\) consists of the sunny lines \\(S_{2},\\dots ,S_{n-1}\\), the vertical line \\(x=1\\), the vertical line \\(x=n\\) and the horizontal line \\(y=n\\).  \n   This family has exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines and, by Lemma 2, covers all points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n   Hence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the impossibility of \\(k=n\\) (Step 2) with the explicit constructions for all \\(k\\le n-1\\) (Step 6) we obtain that the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-1\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
