in\\mathcal V\\).  \n   If \\(b=n\\) the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=n\\).  \n   If \\(b<n\\) set \\(t:=n-b\\) (so \\(1\\le t\\le n-1\\)).  \n   The sunny line \\(S_{t}\\) has equation \\(y = x + (n-t)=x+b\\). Substituting \\(x=a\\) gives \\(y=a+b\\). Because \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); in particular \\(a+b\\le n\\) when \\(b<n\\). Hence \\(a+b\\) equals the required \\(y\\)-coordinate, i.e. \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(S_{t}\\). Thus \\((a,b)\\in S_{t}\\subseteq\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\).\n\n   All possibilities are covered, so every point of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs to a line of \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\). ∎\n\n6. **Realising every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Use \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) from (4).  \n\n   *\\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\).*  Choose any subset \\(\\mathcal V\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) with \\(|\\mathcal V|=k\\) and take the family \\(\\mathcal L(\\mathcal V)\\) given by Lemma 2.  \n\n   In each case the construction supplies exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers all points of \\(T_{n}\\), and contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   From Lemma 1 we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-1\\). Lemma 2 together with the explicit families above shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is attainable. Hence the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-1\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
