or every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the following \\(n\\) lines cover \\(T_n\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_i &: x=i \\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2),\\\\\nL_{n-1}&: y=1,\\\\\nL_{n}&: x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-2\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (one of the \\(L_i\\)) contains the point.\n* If \\(a=n-1\\) then \\(b\\le 2\\).  \n  – When \\(b=1\\) the point lies on \\(y=1\\).  \n  – When \\(b=2\\) we have \\(a+b=n+1\\), so the point lies on \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n* If \\(a=n\\) then necessarily \\(b=1\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)), and the point again lies on \\(y=1\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the listed lines.  \nAll the lines are distinct and each is either vertical, horizontal, or of slope \\(-1\\); consequently none of them is sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (A sunny line meets \\(T_n\\) in at most two points)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a line that is **not** parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nThen \\(|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le 2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in T_n\\).  \nThe vectors \\((a_2-a_1,b_2-b_1)\\) and \\((a_3-a_1,b_3-b_1)\\) are linearly dependent, so there exist integers\n\\(\\lambda,\\mu\\), not both zero, with  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda (a_2-a_1)+\\mu (b_2-b_1)=0 .\n\\]\n\nHence the direction vector of \\(\\ell\\) is orthogonal to \\((\\lambda,\\mu)\\).  \nBecause \\(\\lambda,\\mu\\) are integers, the only possibilities for a direction vector that yields integer lattice points for all three points are proportional to \\((1,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\); i.e. \\(\\ell\\) would be parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\), contradicting the hypothesis. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Upper bound on the number of s