 nor the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nThen \\(|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le 2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in T_n\\).  \nThe vectors \\((a_2-a_1,b_2-b_1)\\) and \\((a_3-a_1,b_3-b_1)\\) are linearly dependent, so there exist integers\n\\(\\lambda,\\mu\\), not both zero, with  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda (a_2-a_1)+\\mu (b_2-b_1)=0 .\n\\]\n\nHence the direction vector of \\(\\ell\\) is orthogonal to \\((\\lambda,\\mu)\\).  \nBecause \\(\\lambda,\\mu\\) are integers, the only possibilities for a direction vector that yields integer lattice points for all three points are proportional to \\((1,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\); i.e. \\(\\ell\\) would be parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\), contradicting the hypothesis. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Upper bound on the number of sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nIf exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny, then  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nPartition \\(\\mathcal L\\) into  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{\\text{horizontal lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\quad\n\\mathcal V=\\{\\text{vertical lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\quad\n\\mathcal D=\\{\\text{lines of slope }-1\\text{ in }\\mathcal L\\},\\quad\n\\mathcal S=\\{\\text{sunny lines in }\\mathcal L\\}.\n\\]\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nh=|\\mathcal H|,\\;v=|\\mathcal V|,\\;d=|\\mathcal D|,\\;k=|\\mathcal S|.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(h+v+d=n-k\\).\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) the three lines  \n\n\\[\nx=a,\\qquad y=b,\\qquad x+y=a+b\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\npass through the point.  \nIf none of the three lines in (1) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\), the point would have to be covered by a sunny line. Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) is either covered by a line from \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\) or by a sunny line.\n\nBy Lemma 2 a sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\); therefore the total number of points that can be left to the sunny lines is at most \\(2k\\).\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|\\;\\le\\;|\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D} (L\\cap T_n)|\\;+\\;2k .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nNow we bound the first term in (2).  \nA horizontal line \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) meets \\(T_n\\) in exactly \\(n+1-c\\) points; the same holds for a vertical line \\(x=c\\).  \nA diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) meets \\(T_n\\) in exactly \\(c-1\\) points.\n\nTherefore, irrespective of which particular horizontal, vertical or diagonal lines are chosen, the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered by all non‑sunny lines together is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{c=1}^{n}(n+1-c)+\\sum_{c=1}^{n}(n+1-c)+\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)\n= n(n+1).\n\\]\n\nInsert this bound into (2) and use \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\;\\le\\; n(n+1)-2k\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Realising every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) that contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nStart with the covering of Lemma 1, which has \\(0\\) sunny lines.  \nFor \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = -(x-i)+(n+1) \\qquad\\text{(i.e. }x+y=n+1+i\\text{)} .\n\\]\n\n\\(S_i\\) passes through the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\,n+1-i),\\qquad Q_i=(i+1,\\,n-i),\n\\]\n\nboth belonging to \\(T_n\\).  \nMoreover \\(S_i\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence \\(S_i\\) is sunny.\n\nReplace the vertical line \\(x=i\\) (present in the original construction) by the sunny line \\(S_i\\).  \nThe points that were covered uniquely by \\(x=i\\) are precisely \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\); after the replacement they are still covered, now by \\(S_i\\).  \nAll other points of \\(T_n\\) remain covered because they lie on one of the unchanged lines \\(x=j\\;(j\\neq i)\\), on \\(y=1\\) or on \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nThus each replacement increases the number of sunny lines by one while preserving the covering property and keeping all lines distinct.  \nPerforming the replacement for the first \\(k\\) values \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) yields a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Result\n\nLemma 3 gives the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\); Lemma 4 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can indeed be realised.  \nConsequently, for any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\,}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
