d \\(2\\)) one can guarantee that the two new lines together still cover all points that were covered by the two removed lines, while not creating any new parallelism with the remaining lines.*\n\nThese three constructions together with the upper bound prove that the only admissible values of \\(k\\) are \\(0,1,2\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a>0,\\;b>0,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe condition of the problem is that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\forall (a,b)\\in S\\;\\exists \\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\;:\\;(a,b)\\in\\ell .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **cloudy**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  At most two sunny lines\n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains three distinct sunny lines\n\\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},\\ell_{3}\\).  Write their slopes as \\(m_{1},m_{2},m_{3}\\) with  \n\n\\[\nm_{i}\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\qquad(i=1,2,3).\n\\]\n\nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_{1}=(1,n),\\qquad P_{2}=(n,1),\\qquad P_{3}=(\\lfloor\\tfrac{n}{2}\\rfloor,\\;\\lfloor\\tfrac{n}{2}\\rfloor).\n\\]\n\nAll three belong to \\(S\\) because \\(1+n\\le n+1\\) and \\(2\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\le n+1\\).  \nBy (1) each \\(P_{i}\\) lies on some line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  Since there are only three sunny lines, by the pigeon‑hole principle at least two of the points are covered by the same sunny line; assume without loss of generality that \\(P_{1},P_{2}\\) lie on \\(\\ell_{1}\\).\n\nThe slope of the line through \\(P_{1}\\) and \\(P_{2}\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-n}{n-1}=-1 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\ell_{1}\\) would have slope \\(-1\\), contradicting the assumption that \\(\\ell_{1}\\) is sunny.  Hence no three sunny lines can coexist, and we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le 2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n-----------------------------------------