\n   Replace the vertical line by a sunny line of slope \\(2\\); it covers all points of the removed vertical line with even \\(y\\)\u2011coordinate, the points with odd \\(y\\) are still covered by the diagonal line that passes through them.  \n   Thus we obtain a configuration with exactly two sunny lines and the covering condition still holds.\n\nTogether the upper bound and the three explicit constructions prove that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely \\(0,1,2\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a>0,\\;b>0,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **cloudy**.  \nWe are given a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n\\[\n\\forall (a,b)\\in S\\;\\exists \\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\;:\\;(a,b)\\in\\ell .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  At most two sunny lines\n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains three distinct sunny lines\n\\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},\\ell_{3}\\).  \nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_{1}=(1,n),\\qquad P_{2}=(n,1),\\qquad P_{3}=\\bigl(\\lfloor\\tfrac{n}{2}\\rfloor,\\;\\lfloor\\tfrac{n}{2}\\rfloor\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nAll three belong to \\(S\\) because \\(1+n\\le n+1\\) and \\(2\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\le n+1\\).  \nBy (1) each \\(P_{i}\\) lies on some line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nSince there are only three sunny lines, by the pigeon\u2011hole principle at least two of the points are covered by the same sunny line; assume without loss of generality that \\(P_{1},P_{2}\\) lie on \\(\\ell_{1}\\).\n\nThe slope of the line through \\(P_{1}\\) and \\(P_{2}\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-n}{\\,n-1\\,}=-1 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(\\ell_{1}\\) would have slope \\(-1\\), contradicting the assumption that \\(\\ell_{1}\\) is sunny.  \nTherefore no three sunny lines can coexist, and we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le 2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A configuration with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) define the three cloudy lines  \n\n\\[\nh_{c}:\\;y=c,\\qquad v_{c}:\\;x=c,\\qquad d_{c}:\\;x+y=c+1 .\n\\]\n\nAll have slopes \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  \nLet \\(\\mathcal C=\\{h_{c},v_{c},d_{c}\\mid 1\\le c\\le n-1\\}\\); then \\(|\\mathcal C|=3(n-1)\\).\n\nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in S\\).  \nExactly one of the equalities \\(b=c\\), \\(a=c\\) or \\(a+b=c+1\\) holds for a unique integer \\(c\\) in the range \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\):\n* if \\(a=b\\) then \\(a+b=2a\\le n+1\\) and \\(c=a-1\\) gives \\(a+b=c+1\\);\n* if \\(a<b\\) then \\(b\\le n-a\\) and \\(c=b\\) gives \\(y=c\\);\n* if \\(a>b\\) then \\(a\\le n-b\\) and \\(c=a\\) gives \\(x=c\\).\n\nThus \\((a,b)\\) lies on exactly one line of \\(\\mathcal C\\).  \nSince \\(3(n-1)\\ge n\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\), we can select any \\(n\\) distinct lines from \\(\\mathcal C\\); the resulting family still covers every point of \\(S\\) and contains no sunny line.  \nHence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for all \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A configuration with exactly one sunny line (\\(k=1\\))\n\nBegin with the family \\(\\mathcal C\\) of \u00a72.  \nChoose a particular horizontal line, say \\(h_{c_{0}}\\) with \\(1\\le c_{0}\\le n-1\\), and delete it.  \nThe points that were covered only by \\(h_{c_{0}}\\) are  \n\n\\[\nH=\\{(a,c_{0})\\mid 1\\le a\\le n-c_{0}\\}.\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(H\\) as the union of two arithmetic progressions according to the parity of the \\(x\\)-coordinate:\n\n\\[\nH_{\\text{odd}}=\\{(2t+1,c_{0})\\mid 0\\le t\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n-c_{0}-1}{2}\\rfloor\\},\n\\qquad\nH_{\\text{even}}=\\{(2t,c_{0})\\mid 1\\le t\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n-c_{0}}{2}\\rfloor\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe vertical line \\(v_{c_{0}}\\) (which we keep) contains all points of \\(H_{\\text{even}}\\) because it passes through \\((c_{0},c_{0})\\) and the slope \\(\\infty\\) makes the \\(y\\)-coordinate constant.\n\nNow introduce a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) of slope \\(\\frac12\\) passing through the two points \\((1,c_{0})\\) and \\((3,c_{0})\\); its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-c_{0}=\\tfrac12\\,(x-1).\n\\]\n\nSince the step in \\(x\\) is \\(2\\) while the step in \\(y\\) is \\(1\\), \\(\\ell\\) contains precisely the points of \\(H_{\\text{odd}}\\).  \nConsequently every point of \\(H\\) is still covered (odd points by \\(\\ell\\), even points by \\(v_{c_{0}}\\)).  \n\nAll other points of \\(S\\) are untouched, because they lie on lines of \\(\\mathcal C\\) different from \\(h_{c_{0}}\\).  \nThus the new family, consisting of the \\(n-1\\) remaining lines of \\(\\mathcal C\\) together with \\(\\ell\\), satisfies (1) and contains exactly one sunny line.  \n\nHence a configuration with \\(k=1\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  A configuration with exactly two sunny lines (\\(k=2\\))\n\nAgain start from \\(\\mathcal C\\).  \nRemove one horizontal line \\(h_{c_{1}}\\) and one vertical line \\(v_{c_{2}}\\) with \\(c_{1}\\neq c_{2}\\) (both lie in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\)).  \n\n*Points formerly on \\(h_{c_{1}}\\).*  \nThey are \\(H_{1}=\\{(a,c_{1})\\mid 1\\le a\\le n-c_{1}\\}\\).  \nDefine the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{1}:\\; y-c_{1}= \\tfrac12\\,(x-1) .\n\\]\n\nAs in \u00a73, \\(\\ell_{1}\\) contains all points of \\(H_{1}\\) with odd \\(x\\); the points of \\(H_{1}\\) with even \\(x\\) are still covered by the diagonal line \\(d_{c_{1}}\\) (which passes through \\((c_{1},1)\\) and \\((1,c_{1})\\)).\n\n*Points formerly on \\(v_{c_{2}}\\).*  \nThey are \\(V_{2}=\\{(c_{2},b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-c_{2}\\}\\).  \nDefine the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{2}:\\; y-(n-c_{2}) = 2\\,(x-n) .\n\\]\n\nEquivalently, \\(\\ell_{2}\\) passes through the two points \\((n,c_{2})\\) and \\((n-1,c_{2}+2)\\); its slope is \\(2\\).  \nBecause the step in \\(y\\) is \\(2\\) while the step in \\(x\\) is \\(1\\), \\(\\ell_{2}\\) contains exactly the points of \\(V_{2}\\) with even \\(y\\); the points of \\(V_{2}\\) with odd \\(y\\) are still covered by the diagonal line \\(d_{c_{2}}\\) (which passes through \\((c_{2},1)\\) and \\((1,c_{2})\\)).\n\nAll other points of \\(S\\) remain on the untouched lines of \\(\\mathcal C\\).  \nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(\\mathcal C\\setminus\\{h_{c_{1}},v_{c_{2}}\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly two sunny lines, consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, and satisfies the covering condition (1).\n\nThus a configuration with \\(k=2\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nFrom (2) we have the universal bound \\(k\\le 2\\).  \nSections 2, 3 and 4 provide explicit constructions for \\(k=0,1,2\\) for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2\\}} .\n\\]"
