-b_1|\\le |a_2-a_1|\\) and the only integer slopes possible are \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nThus any two distinct points of \\(S\\) on the same line would force the line to be parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, contradicting the definition of “sunny”. ∎\n\n\n\n### Lemma 2  \nA line that is **not** sunny (i.e. parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\)) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the three possible directions.\n\n* **Horizontal line** \\(y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\ge 1\\).  \n  Points of \\(S\\) on this line satisfy \\(b=c\\) and \\(a\\le n+1-c\\); therefore there are at most \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) such points.\n\n* **Vertical line** \\(x=c\\) is symmetric and also contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n* **Line of slope \\(-1\\)**: \\(x+y=d\\) with integer \\(d\\ge 2\\).  \n  Points of \\(S\\) on this line satisfy \\(a+b=d\\) and \\(d\\le n+1\\); the number of positive integer solutions of \\(a+b=d\\) is \\(d-1\\le n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n### Main Argument  \n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L_{\\text{sunny}}\\) be the set of sunny lines and let \\(\\ell_{\\text{bad}}\\) be the unique non‑sunny line.\n\nBy Lemma 1 each line in \\(\\mathcal L_{\\text{sunny}}\\) covers at most one point of \\(S\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L_{\\text{sunny}}}(L\\cap S)\\bigr|\\le n-1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma 2 the line \\(\\ell_{\\text{bad}}\\) covers at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\); thus  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\ell_{\\text{bad}}\\cap S\\,|\\le n. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) must cover **all** points of \\(S\\),\n\n\\[\n|S|\\le (n-1)+n = 2n-1. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBut from (1) we have  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>2n-1\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge 3,\n\\]\n\nbecause  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(2n-1)=\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{2}\\ge 0,\n\\]\n\nwith strict inequality for all \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nThus (4) is impossible, contradicting the assumed existence of such a configuration.\n\nTherefore **no** configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition can have exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
