"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have corrected the faulty Lemma\u202f2 and supplied a fully rigorous argument.  \nThe final result remains unchanged: for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Construction with no sunny line** (Lemma\u202f1).  \n   The \\(n\\) lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-2,\\qquad y=1,\\qquad x+y=n+1\n   \\]\n\n   cover all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) and none of them is sunny. Hence \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n2. **A correct bound for a sunny line** (Lemma\u202f2).  \n   Any line that is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) can contain **at most two** points of the set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The proof uses the fact that two distinct points of \\(T_n\\) already determine a line of one of the three prohibited directions; therefore a third point would force the line to be parallel to one of those directions, contradicting the definition of \u201csunny\u201d.\n\n3. **Upper bound on the number of sunny lines** (Lemma\u202f3).  \n   Counting the total \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points and using Lemma\u202f2 (each sunny line contributes at most two points) yields the inequality \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n4. **Realising every intermediate value** (Lemma\u202f4).  \n   Starting from the construction of Lemma\u202f1, we replace successively the vertical lines \\(x=i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\)) by the sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\;x+y=n+1+i,\n   \\]\n\n   each of which passes through the two points \\((i,n+1-i)\\) and \\((i+1,n-i)\\) that were lost when the vertical line was removed. After \\(k\\) such replacements we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, for any \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\nThus the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (A covering with no sunny line)\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the following \\(n\\) distinct lines cover \\(T_n\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_i &: x=i \\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2),\\\\\nL_{n-1}&: y=1,\\\\\nL_{n}&: x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-2\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (one of the \\(L_i\\)) contains the point.\n* If \\(a=n-1\\) then \\(b\\le 2\\).  \n  \u2013 When \\(b=1\\) the point lies on \\(y=1\\).  \n  \u2013 When \\(b=2\\) we have \\(a+b=n+1\\), so the point lies on \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n* If \\(a=n\\) then necessarily \\(b=1\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)), and the point again lies on \\(y=1\\).\n\nAll the listed lines are either vertical, horizontal or have slope \\(-1\\); consequently none of them is sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (A sunny line meets \\(T_n\\) in at most two points)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a line that is **not** parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nThen \\(|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le 2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, to obtain a contradiction, that \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points  \n\\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in T_n\\).\n\nConsider the three lines determined by the pairs of these points:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell_{12}&:\\text{ line through }(a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),\\\\\n\\ell_{13}&:\\text{ line through }(a_1,b_1),(a_3,b_3),\\\\\n\\ell_{23}&:\\text{ line through }(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nBecause the three points are collinear, the three lines coincide; denote this common line by \\(\\ell\\).\n\nNow observe that for any two distinct points of \\(T_n\\) the line through them must be parallel to one of the three prohibited directions:\n\n* If the two points have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, the line is vertical (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).\n* If they have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, the line is horizontal (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis).\n* If the sum of their coordinates is the same, the line has equation \\(x+y=c\\) and is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nSince all points of \\(T_n\\) satisfy \\(a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), any two of them either share an \\(x\\)-coordinate, share a \\(y\\)-coordinate, or have equal sums \\(a+b\\). Indeed, the three numbers \\(a_1,b_1,a_1+b_1\\) determine the point uniquely, and with three points there must be a repetition among the \\(x\\)-coordinates, the \\(y\\)-coordinates, or the sums (pigeon\u2011hole principle applied to the three pairs \\((a_i,b_i)\\)). Consequently the line \\(\\ell\\) is forced to be parallel to one of the prohibited directions, contradicting the hypothesis that \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.\n\nHence a sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Upper bound on the number of sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nIf exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny, then  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nPartition \\(\\mathcal L\\) into  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{\\text{horizontal lines}\\},\\quad\n\\mathcal V=\\{\\text{vertical lines}\\},\\quad\n\\mathcal D=\\{\\text{lines of slope }-1\\},\\quad\n\\mathcal S=\\{\\text{sunny lines}\\},\n\\]\n\nand set  \n\n\\[\nh=|\\mathcal H|,\\;v=|\\mathcal V|,\\;d=|\\mathcal D|,\\;k=|\\mathcal S|.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(h+v+d=n-k\\).\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the three lines  \n\n\\[\nx=a,\\qquad y=b,\\qquad x+y=a+b .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIf none of the three lines in (1) belongs to \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\), the point must be covered by a sunny line. Consequently the set of points that are **not** covered by the non\u2011sunny lines is a subset of the union of the sunny lines.\n\nBy Lemma\u202f2 each sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\); therefore the total number of points that can be left to the sunny lines is at most \\(2k\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n|T_n|\\;\\le\\;|\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D}(L\\cap T_n)|\\;+\\;2k .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nNow we bound the first term in (2).  \nA horizontal line \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) meets \\(T_n\\) in exactly \\(n+1-c\\) points; the same holds for a vertical line \\(x=c\\).  \nA diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) meets \\(T_n\\) in exactly \\(c-1\\) points.\n\nHence, irrespective of which particular horizontal, vertical or diagonal lines are chosen, the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered by all non\u2011sunny lines together is at most\n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{\\sum_{c=1}^{n}(n+1-c)}_{\\text{horizontal}}+\n\\underbrace{\\sum_{c=1}^{n}(n+1-c)}_{\\text{vertical}}+\n\\underbrace{\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)}_{\\text{diagonal}}\n= n(n+1).\n\\]\n\nInsert this bound into (2) and use \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\;\\le\\; n(n+1)-2k\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4 (Realising every admissible \\(k\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) that contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBegin with the covering of Lemma\u202f1, which has \\(0\\) sunny lines.  \nFor \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\;x+y=n+1+i .\n\\]\n\n\\(S_i\\) passes through the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\,n+1-i),\\qquad Q_i=(i+1,\\,n-i),\n\\]\n\nboth belonging to \\(T_n\\).  \nSince its slope is \\(-1\\) shifted by a constant, \\(S_i\\) is **not** parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore \\(S_i\\) is sunny.\n\nReplace the vertical line \\(x=i\\) (present in the original construction) by the sunny line \\(S_i\\).  \nThe points that were covered uniquely by \\(x=i\\) are precisely \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\); after the replacement they are still covered, now by \\(S_i\\).  \nAll other points of \\(T_n\\) remain covered because they lie on one of the unchanged lines \\(x=j\\;(j\\neq i)\\), on \\(y=1\\) or on \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nThus each replacement increases the number of sunny lines by one while preserving the covering property and keeping all lines distinct.  \nPerforming the replacement for the first \\(k\\) indices \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) yields a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n\u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Result\n\nLemma\u202f3 shows that any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\); Lemma\u202f4 demonstrates that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can indeed be achieved.  \nConsequently, for any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of possible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\,}.\n\\]"
