s of \\(T_n\\) the line through them must be parallel to one of the three prohibited directions:\n\n* If the two points have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, the line is vertical (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).\n* If they have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, the line is horizontal (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis).\n* If the sum of their coordinates is the same, the line has equation \\(x+y=c\\) and is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nSince all points of \\(T_n\\) satisfy \\(a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), any two of them either share an \\(x\\)-coordinate, share a \\(y\\)-coordinate, or have equal sums \\(a+b\\). Indeed, the three numbers \\(a_1,b_1,a_1+b_1\\) determine the point uniquely, and with three points there must be a repetition among the \\(x\\)-coordinates, the \\(y\\)-coordinates, or the sums (pigeon‑hole principle applied to the three pairs \\((a_i,b_i)\\)). Consequently the line \\(\\ell\\) is forced to be parallel to one of the prohibited directions, contradicting the hypothesis that \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.\n\nHence a sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Upper bound on the number of sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nIf exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny, then  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nPartition \\(\\mathcal L\\) into  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{\\text{horizontal lines}\\},\\quad\n\\mathcal V=\\{\\text{vertical lines}\\},\\quad\n\\mathcal D=\\{\\text{lines of slope }-1\\},\\quad\n\\mathcal S=\\{\\text{sunny lines}\\},\n\\]\n\nand set  \n\n\\[\nh=|\\mathcal H|,\\;v=|\\mathcal V|,\\;d=|\\mathcal D|,\\;k=|\\mathcal S|.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(h+v+d=n-k\\).\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the three lines  \n\n\\[\nx=a,\\qquad y=b,\\qquad x+y=a+b .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIf none of the three lines in (1) belongs to \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\), the point must be covered by a sunny line. Consequently the set of points that are **not** covered by the non‑sunny lines is a subset of the union of the sunny lines.\n\nBy Lemma 2 each sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\); therefore the total number of points that can be left to the sunny lines is at most \\(2k\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n|T_n|\\;\\le\\;|\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D}(L\\cap T_n)|\\;+\\;2k .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nNow we bound the first term in (2).  \nA horizontal line \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) meets \\(T_n\\) in exactly \\(n+1-c\\) points; the same holds for a vertical line \\(x=c\\).  \nA diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) meets \\(T_n\\) in exactly \\(c-1\\) points.\n\nHence, irrespective of which particular horizontal, vertical or diagonal lines are chosen, the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered by all non‑sunny lines together is at most\n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{\\sum_{c=1}^{n}(n+1-c)}_{\\text{horizontal}}+\n\\underbrace{\\sum_{c=1}^{n}(n+1-c)}_{\\text{vertical}}+\n\\underbrace{\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)}_{\\text{diagonal}}\n= n(n+1).\n\\]\n\nInsert this bound into (2) and use \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\;\\le\\; n(n+1)-2k\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Realising every admissible \\(k\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) that contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBegin with the covering of Lemma 1, which has \\(0\\) sunny lines.  \nFor \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\;x+y=n+1+i .\n\\]\n\n\\(S_i\\) passes through the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\,n+1-i),\\qquad Q_i=(i+1,\\,n-i),\n\\]\n\nboth belonging to \\(T_n\\).  \nSince its slope is \\(-1\\) shifted by a constant, \\(S_i\\) is **not** parallel to the