) and \\((i+1,n-i)\\) that were lost when the vertical line was removed. After \\(k\\) such replacements we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, for any \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\nThus the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (A covering with no sunny line)\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the following \\(n\\) distinct lines cover \\(T_n\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_i &: x=i \\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2),\\\\\nL_{n-1}&: y=1,\\\\\nL_{n}&: x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-2\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (one of the \\(L_i\\)) contains the point.\n* If \\(a=n-1\\) then \\(b\\le 2\\).  \n  \u2013 When \\(b=1\\) the point lies on \\(y=1\\).  \n  \u2013 When \\(b=2\\) we have \\(a+b=n+1\\), so the point lies on \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n* If \\(a=n\\) then necessarily \\(b=1\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)), and the point again lies on \\(y=1\\).\n\nAll the listed lines are vertical, horizontal or have slope \\(-1\\); consequently none of them is sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (A sunny line meets \\(T_n\\) in at most \\(n-1\\) points)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a line that is **not** parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nThen \\(|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le n-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) in the form \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\neq0,-1\\).  \nFor each integer \\(y\\) with \\(1\\le y\\le n\\) the equation \\(y=mx+b\\) has at most one solution for \\(x\\); hence \\(\\ell\\) meets each horizontal line \\(y=c\\) (\\(c\\in\\{1,\\d