no sunny line)\n\nThe following \\(n\\) distinct lines cover \\(T_n\\):\n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\;(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2),\\qquad\nL_{n-1}:\\;y=1,\\qquad\nL_{n}:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).\n* If \\(a=n-1\\) then \\(b\\le2\\).  \n  – When \\(b=1\\) the point lies on \\(y=1\\).  \n  – When \\(b=2\\) we have \\(a+b=n+1\\), so the point lies on \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n* If \\(a=n\\) then necessarily \\(b=1\\), again on \\(y=1\\).\n\nAll listed lines are vertical, horizontal or have slope \\(-1\\); none is sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Maximum number of points of \\(T_n\\) on a sunny line)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a line that is **not** parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with reduced rational slope \\(m=p/q\\) (\\(q\\ge1\\), \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\)) and \\(m\\neq0,-1\\).\n\nFor an integer abscissa \\(a\\) the ordinate is \\(ma+b\\).  \nBecause \\(m\\) is rational, \\(ma+b\\) is an integer exactly when \\(a\\equiv a_{0}\\pmod q\\) for a certain residue class \\(a_{0}\\).  \nHence among the integers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\) at most one out of each block of \\(q\\) consecutive values can give a point of \\(\\ell\\cap T_n\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{q}\\Bigr\\rceil .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(q\\ge2\\) then the right‑hand side of (1) is at most \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\), and the lemma follows.  \nThus it suffices to treat the case \\(q=1\\), i.e. an **integer** slope \\(m\\neq0,-1\\).\n\nAssume \\(m\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \nFrom \\(b=ma+b_{0}\\) with integer \\(b_{0}\\) we obtain, using the defining inequality of \\(T_n\\),\n\n\\[\na+ma+b_{0}\\le n+1\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;(1+m)a\\le n+1-b_{0}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(m>0\\).* Then \\(1+m\\ge2\\) and the right‑hand side of (2) is at most \\(n\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\na\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{1+m}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThus at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) integer values of \\(a\\) satisfy (2).\n\n*If \\(m<-1\\).* Then \\(1+m\\le-1\\) and (2) gives a lower bound for \\(a\\); writing \\(a'=-a\\) reduces this case to the previous one and yields the same upper bound \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\nHence in every possible situation  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\qquad\\Box\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Upper bound for the number of sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\), and let exactly \\(k\\) of them be sunny.  \nThen \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nPartition \\(\\mathcal L\\) into the sunny subset \\(\\mathcal S\\) (\\(|\\mathcal S|=k\\)) and the non‑sunny subset \\(\\mathcal N\\) (\\(|\\mathcal N|=n-k\\)).  \n\n* For a sunny line \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal S\\) Lemma 2 gives \\(|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n* A non‑sunny line is either vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\); each of these three types can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (e.g. the vertical line \\(x=1\\) contains the \\(n\\) points \\((1,1),\\dots ,(1,n)\\)).\n\nTherefore\n\n\\[\n|T_n|\n\\;\\le\\;\n(n-k)\\,n\\;+\\;k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\), inequality (3) becomes\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n^{2}-kn+k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nRearranging yields\n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\frac{2n^{2}-n(n+1)}{2n-2\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor}\n=\n\\frac{n-1}{1-\\dfrac{\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor}{n}} .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\ge \\dfrac{n}{2}\\), the denominator is at most \\(\\tfrac12\\); consequently\n\n\\[\nk\\le 2\\,(n-1)-1 = n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus any feasible configuration satisfies \\(k\\le n-2\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n-2]\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) that contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nStart from the covering of Lemma 1:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{0}= \\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\;y=(i-1)x-(i-1)i+(n+1).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_i\\) is the integer \\(i-1\\); since \\(i-1\\neq0,-1\\), each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  \nA direct substitution shows that \\(S_i\\) passes through the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\,n+1-i),\\qquad Q_i=(i+1,\\,n-i),\n\\]\n\nwhich are precisely the points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on the vertical line \\(x=i\\) in \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\).\n\nReplace, for the first \\(k\\) indices, the vertical line \\(x=i\\) by the sunny line \\(S_i\\).  \nThe resulting set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{k}= \\bigl(\\mathcal L_{0}\\setminus\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=k\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\n\\]\n\nstill consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, still covers every point of \\(T_n\\) (the points lost from each removed vertical line are recovered on the corresponding \\(S_i\\)), and now contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Conclusion\n\nLemma 3 shows that any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nLemma 4 demonstrates that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) such a configuration exists.  \n\nHence the set of possible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\,}.\n\\]"
