ny line)\n\nThe following \\(n\\) distinct lines cover \\(T_n\\):\n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\;(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2),\\qquad\nL_{n-1}:\\;y=1,\\qquad\nL_{n}:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).\n* If \\(a=n-1\\) then \\(b\\le2\\).  \n  – When \\(b=1\\) the point lies on \\(y=1\\).  \n  – When \\(b=2\\) we have \\(a+b=n+1\\), so the point lies on \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n* If \\(a=n\\) then necessarily \\(b=1\\), again on \\(y=1\\).\n\nAll listed lines are vertical, horizontal or have slope \\(-1\\); none is sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Maximum number of points of \\(T_n\\) on a sunny line)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a line that is **not** parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with reduced rational slope \\(m=p/q\\) (\\(q\\ge1\\), \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\)) and \\(m\\neq0,-1\\).\n\nFor an integer abscissa \\(a\\) the ordinate is \\(ma+b\\).  \nBecause \\(m\\) is rational, \\(ma+b\\) is an integer exactly when \\(a\\equiv a_{0}\\pmod q\\) for a certain residue class \\(a_{0}\\).  \nHence among the integers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\) at most one out of each block of \\(q\\) consecutive values can give a point of \\(\\ell\\cap T_n\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{q}\\Bigr\\rceil .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(q\\ge2\\) then the right‑hand side of (1) is at most \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\), and the lemma follows.  \nThus it suffices to treat the case \\(q=1\\), i.e. an **integer** slope \\(m\\neq0,-1\\).\n\nAssume \\(m\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \nFrom \\(b=ma+b_{0}\\) with integer \\(b_{0}\\) we obtain, using the defining inequality of \\(T_n\\),\n\n\\[\na+ma+b_{0}\\le n+1\\;\\Longrightar