  \n\n   Replacing, for the first \\(k\\) indices, the vertical line \\(x=i\\) by the sunny line \\(S_i\\) yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that still covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and now contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   Because \\(k\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n-2\\), every value in that range is attainable.\n\n6. **Conclusion**.  \n   Lemma 3 gives the universal upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\); Lemma 4 shows that each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can indeed be achieved.  \n   Hence the set of possible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (A covering with no sunny line)\n\nThe \\(n\\) distinct lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\;(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2),\\qquad\nL_{n-1}:\\;y=1,\\qquad\nL_{n}:\\;x+y=n+1\n\\]\n\ncover every point of \\(T_n\\).  \nAll of them are vertical, horizontal or have slope \\(-1\\); therefore none is sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Points of \\(T_n\\) on a sunny line)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a line that is **not** parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with reduced rational slope \\(m=p/q\\) (\\(q\\ge1\\), \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\)) and \\(m\\neq0,-1\\).\n\nFor an integer abscissa \\(a\\) the ordinate equals \\(ma+b\\).  \nBecause \\(m\\) is rational, \\(ma+b\\) is an integer exactly when \\(a\\equiv a_{0}\\pmod q\\) for a certain residue class \\(a_{0}\\).  \nHence among the integers \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) at most one out of each block of \\(q\\) consecutive values can give a point of \\(\\ell\\cap T_n\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{q}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(q\\ge2\\) then \\(\\lceil n/q\\rceil\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) and we are done.  \nThus it suffices to treat the case \\(q=1\\), i.e. an **integer** slope \\(m\\neq0,-1\\).\n\nWrite \\(b=ma_0+b_0\\) with integers \\(a_0,b_0\\).  \nFor a point \\((a,ma+b_0)\\) to belong to \\(T_n\\) we need  \n\n\\[\na+ma+b_0\\le n+1\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;(1+m)a\\le n+1-b_0 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(m>0\\)* then \\(1+m\\ge2\\) and the right‑hand side of (2) is at most \\(n\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\na\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{1+m}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n*If \\(m<-1\\)* then \\(1+m\\le-1\\); rewriting (2) as \\((-1-m)a\\le n+1-b_0\\) reduces to the previous case and yields the same bound.  \n\nThus in every situation  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{★}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (At least two non‑sunny lines are necessary)\n\nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nA=(1,1),\\qquad B=(n,1),\\qquad C=(1,n).\n\\]\n\nThe lines \\(AB\\), \\(AC\\) and \\(BC\\) are respectively horizontal, vertical and of slope \\(-1\\); none of them is sunny.  \nHence a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the points \\(A,B,C\\).\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all of \\(T_n\\) and contains at most one non‑sunny line; i.e. \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n* The unique non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (the maximum is attained by a vertical line \\(x=1\\), a horizontal line \\(y=1\\) or the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\)).\n* Each sunny line contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\) by (★).\n\nTherefore the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nn+(n-1)\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA direct computation shows that for every \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\nn+(n-1)\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n<\\frac{n(n+1)}2=|T_n|.\n\\]\n\nIndeed,\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nn\\text{ even }(n=2t):\\;&\nn+(n-1)\\frac{n}{2}=2t+(2t-1)t=\\frac{2t^{2}+2t-1}{ }<\\frac{2t^{2}+2t}{ }=\\frac{n(n+1)}2,\\\\\nn\\text{ odd }(n=2t+1):\\;&\nn+(n-1)\\frac{n+1}{2}=2t+1+2t\\,(t+1)=\\frac{2t^{2}+4t+1}{ }<\\frac{(2t+1)(2t+2)}2=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus with at most one non‑sunny line the set \\(T_n\\) cannot be completely covered, contradicting the assumption.  \nConsequently **at least two lines of any covering must be non‑sunny**, i.e.\n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge2\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{★★}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\))\n\nStart from the covering of Lemma 1:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{0}= \\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\;y=(i-1)x-(i-1)i+(n+1).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_i\\) is the integer \\(i-1\\neq0,-1\\); hence \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  \nA short substitution shows that  \n\n\\[\nS_i\\text{ passes through }(i,n+1-i)\\text{ and }(i+1,n-i),\n\\]\n\nthe two points of \\(T_n\\) that were previously covered by the vertical line \\(x=i\\).\n\nReplace, for the first \\(k\\) indices, the vertical line \\(x=i\\) by the sunny line \\(S_i\\).  \nThe resulting family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{k}= \\bigl(\\mathcal L_{0}\\setminus\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=k\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\n\\]\n\nstill consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, still covers every point of \\(T_n\\), and now contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nSince \\(k\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n-2\\), every value in that interval is realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Conclusion\n\nLemma 3 establishes the universal bound \\(k\\le n-2\\); Lemma 4 demonstrates that each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can indeed be achieved.  \nTherefore the set of possible numbers of sunny lines for the given problem is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
