placed vertical line originally covered a whole column of points, we must ensure that the new sunny line still passes through all points of that column that are required.  \n   This is achieved by letting the sunny line pass through the two extreme points of the column, namely \\((i,1)\\) and \\((i,n+1-i)\\); the line determined by these two points has slope \\(-\\dfrac{n}{i}\\), which is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) for \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\).  \n   Hence each replacement yields a sunny line that still covers every point previously covered by the removed vertical line.  \n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\).**  \n   Suppose a configuration with \\(n\\) lines contains at least \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \n   Since a sunny line cannot be parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, each sunny line can contain at most **two** points of \\(S_n\\) (otherwise two of its points would have the same \\(x\\)\u2011 or \\(y\\)\u2011coordinate or would lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   Consequently the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines together can cover at most \\(2(n-1)\\) points.  \n   The set \\(S_n\\) contains \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\\) points, which for \\(n\\ge 3\\) satisfies  \n   \\[\n   \\frac{(n-1)n}{2}>2(n-1)\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad n>4 .\n   \\]\n   For \\(n=3,4\\) a direct inspection shows that at least two non\u2011sunny lines are required to cover the three (resp. six) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   Hence a configuration with \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines cannot exist for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nThe lemmas together give the exact set of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|S_n|=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f1 (Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\))\n\nDefine the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_i &: x=i \\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2),\\\\\nD_1 &: x+y=n,\\\\\nD_2 &: x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll of them are non\u2011sunny (vertical or slope \\(-1\\)).  \nWe claim that \\(\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n-2}L_i\\cup D_1\\cup D_2\\) contains every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  \nIf \\(a\\le n-2\\) then the point lies on the vertical line \\(L_a\\).  \nIf \\(a=n-1\\) then \\(b\\le 2\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)), so \\(a+b=n+1\\) and the point lies on \\(D_2\\).  \nIf \\(a=n\\) then \\(b=1\\) and again \\(a+b=n+1\\), so the point lies on \\(D_2\\).  \nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered.  \nThe lines are distinct, so we have a configuration with exactly \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f2 (Construction for any \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\))\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nStart from the configuration of Lemma\u202f1 and replace the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{i_1},L_{i_2},\\dots ,L_{i_k}\\qquad (1\\le i_1<\\dots <i_k\\le n-2)\n\\]\n\nby the \\(k\\) lines\n\n\\[\nS_j:\\; \\text{the line through }(i_j,1)\\text{ and }(i_j,n+1-i_j)\\quad (j=1,\\dots ,k).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_j\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_j=\\frac{(n+1-i_j)-1}{i_j-i_j}= -\\frac{n}{i_j},\n\\]\n\nwhich is finite, non\u2011zero and different from \\(-1\\) because \\(i_j\\ge 1\\) and \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nHence each \\(S_j\\) is sunny.\n\nBoth points \\((i_j,1)\\) and \\((i_j,n+1-i_j)\\) belong to \\(S_n\\); consequently the whole segment of \\(S_j\\) between them lies inside the triangle that contains \\(S_n\\).  \nEvery point of the column \\(x=i_j\\) that belongs to \\(S_n\\) is a convex combination of these two extreme points, and therefore also lies on \\(S_j\\).  \nThus the replacement does not lose any covered point.\n\nAll other lines (\\(L_i\\) with \\(i\\notin\\{i_1,\\dots ,i_k\\}\\) and the two diagonals \\(D_1,D_2\\)) remain unchanged, so the resulting family of \\(n\\) distinct lines still covers all of \\(S_n\\).  \nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_j\\) are sunny, giving a configuration with the prescribed number of sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f3 (Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\))\n\nAssume a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(S_n\\) and contains at least \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \nConsider any sunny line \\(\\ell\\).  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) contained three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in S_n\\), then at least two of these points would share either the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, or satisfy \\(a_i+b_i=a_j+b_j\\).  \nIn each of those three cases the line through the two points would be respectively vertical, horizontal, or of slope \\(-1\\), contradicting the definition of a sunny line.  \nHence **every sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(S_n\\).**\n\nIf there are \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, they together cover at most \\(2(n-1)\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \nThe remaining line (which may be non\u2011sunny) can cover at most all points of a single column, a single row, or a single diagonal, i.e. at most \\(n-1\\) points.  \nConsequently the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1)+(n-1)=3n-3 .\n\\]\n\nBut  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}>3n-3\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge 5 .\n\\]\n\nThus for \\(n\\ge5\\) a configuration with \\(n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist.  \n\nFor the remaining small values \\(n=3,4\\) we check directly:\n\n* \\(n=3\\): \\(S_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \n  Any sunny line can contain at most two of these points, so at least one non\u2011sunny line is necessary; hence \\(k\\le 2=n-1\\) is impossible.\n\n* \\(n=4\\): \\(S_4\\) has six points.  \n  Six points cannot be covered by three sunny lines (each at most two points) together with one additional line, because the extra line can cover at most three point