ma 1 and replace the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{i_1},L_{i_2},\\dots ,L_{i_k}\\qquad (1\\le i_1<\\dots <i_k\\le n-2)\n\\]\n\nby the \\(k\\) lines\n\n\\[\nS_j:\\; \\text{the line through }(i_j,1)\\text{ and }(i_j,n+1-i_j)\\quad (j=1,\\dots ,k).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_j\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_j=\\frac{(n+1-i_j)-1}{i_j-i_j}= -\\frac{n}{i_j},\n\\]\n\nwhich is finite, non‑zero and different from \\(-1\\) because \\(i_j\\ge 1\\) and \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nHence each \\(S_j\\) is sunny.\n\nBoth points \\((i_j,1)\\) and \\((i_j,n+1-i_j)\\) belong to \\(S_n\\); consequently the whole segment of \\(S_j\\) between them lies inside the triangle that contains \\(S_n\\).  \nEvery point of the column \\(x=i_j\\) that belongs to \\(S_n\\) is a convex combination of these two extreme points, and therefore also lies on \\(S_j\\).  \nThus the replacement does not lose any covered point.\n\nAll other lines (\\(L_i\\) with \\(i\\notin\\{i_1,\\dots ,i_k\\}\\) and the two diagonals \\(D_1,D_2\\)) remain unchanged, so the resulting family of \\(n\\) distinct lines still covers all of \\(S_n\\).  \nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_j\\) are sunny, giving a configuration with the prescribed number of sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\))\n\nAssume a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(S_n\\) and contains at least \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \n\nA sunny line is not parallel to any side of the triangle \\(\\{a\\ge1,b\\ge1,a+b\\le n+1\\}\\).  \nHence it meets each side of the triangle in at most one point.  \nConsequently a sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(S_n\\) (the two intersection points with the boundary).  \n\nTherefore the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(2(n-1)\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \nThe remaining line (which may be non‑sunny) can be vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\); in any case it can contain at most all points of a single column, row or diagonal, i.e. at most \\(n-1\\) points.  \n\nHence the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1)+(n-1)=3n-3 .\n\\]\n\nBut  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}>3n-3\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge 5 .\n\\]\n\nThus for \\(n\\ge5\\) a configuration with \\(n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist.  \n\nFor the remaining small values we check directly:\n\n* \\(n=3\\): \\(S_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \n  Any sunny line can contain at most two of these points, so at least one non‑sunny line is necessary; hence \\(k=2=n-1\\) is impossible.\n\n* \\(n=4\\): \\(S_4\\) has six points.  \n  Six points cannot be covered by three sunny lines (each at most two points) together with one additional line, because the extra line can cover at most three points (a whole column or row).  \n  Hence \\(k=3=n-1\\) is impossible.\n\nIf all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, the same argument gives an upper bound of \\(2n\\) covered points, which is smaller than \\(|S_n|\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\); therefore \\(k=n\\) is also impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion  \n\nLemma 1 provides a configuration with \\(k=0\\).  \nLemma 2 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.  \nLemma 3 proves that \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) cannot occur.  \n\nHence the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which a configuration exists is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
