"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to obtain a complete classification of all admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following:\n\n*For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the existence of a covering by \\(n\\) distinct lines with **no** sunny line (\\(k=0\\)) is equivalent to the parity of \\(n\\): such a covering exists **iff** \\(n\\) is even. Moreover, an explicit construction for even \\(n\\) is given, and for odd \\(n\\) a proof shows that at least one sunny line is necessary.*\n\nThus the problem is completely solved for the case \\(k=0\\); the remaining values \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) are still open.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Classification of non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   By definition a non\u2011sunny line is parallel to one of the three distinguished directions, hence its slope is either \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\).\n\n2. **Notation.**  \n   For a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines let  \n   \\[\n   h=\\#\\{\\text{horizontal lines}\\},\\quad\n   v=\\#\\{\\text{vertical lines}\\},\\quad\n   d=\\#\\{\\text{lines of slope }-1\\},\\quad\n   s=\\#\\{\\text{sunny lines}\\}.\n   \\]\n   Then \\(h+v+d+s=n\\).\n\n3. **Covering condition when \\(s=0\\).**  \n   For any lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) the three lines\n   \\[\n   y=b,\\qquad x=a,\\qquad x+y=a+b\n   \\]\n   all pass through \\((a,b)\\). If no sunny line is present, at least one of these three lines must belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n4. **Counting argument for \\(s=0\\).**  \n   Consider the \u201ccentral square\u2019\u2019  \n   \\[\n   A=\\Bigl\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\;\\Big|\\;1\\le a,b\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}.\n   \\]\n   Every point of \\(A\\) can be covered only by a horizontal line \\(y=b\\) or a vertical line \\(x=a\\); the line of slope \\(-1\\) through such a point has intercept \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) but would be redundant because the horizontal or vertical line already exists. Consequently each integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\) must appear as the ordinate of at least one line in \\(\\mathcal L\\), or each integer \\(a\\) with the same range must appear as the abscissa of at least one line. Hence\n   \\[\n   h+v\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\\tag{\u2605}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Parity obstruction.**  \n   If \\(n\\) is odd, write \\(n=2m+1\\). Then (\u2605) gives \\(h+v\\ge m+1\\). Since \\(h+v+d=n\\) and \\(s=0\\), we have \\(d\\le m\\). The point \\((m+1,m+1)\\) belongs to the required set because \\(2(m+1)=2m+2=n+1\\). Its three covering lines are \\(y=m+1\\), \\(x=m+1\\) and \\(x+y=2m+2\\). The first two would require a horizontal and a vertical line with index \\(m+1\\), but at most \\(m\\) lines of each type are available (otherwise \\(h+v\\) would exceed \\(m+1\\) and force \\(d<0\\)). The third line would be a slope\u2011\\(-1\\) line with intercept \\(2m+2\\), but we have at most \\(m\\) such lines. Hence none of the three possibilities is present, contradicting the covering requirement. Therefore for odd \\(n\\) a covering with \\(s=0\\) cannot exist.\n\n6. **Explicit construction for even \\(n\\).**  \n   Let \\(n=2m\\). Define\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\n   \\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=m\\,\\}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\{\\,x=1,\\,x=2,\\dots ,x=m\\,\\}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\{\\,x+y=m+2,\\,x+y=m+3,\\dots ,x+y=2m\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n   This family contains \\(m\\) horizontal, \\(m\\) vertical and \\(m\\) slope\u2011\\(-1\\) lines, altogether \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   For any \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1=2m+1\\) we have:\n   * If \\(b\\le m\\) the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\);\n   * If \\(b>m\\) then \\(a\\le 2m+1-b\\le m\\), so the vertical line \\(x=a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n   Hence every required lattice point is covered.\n\n7. **Conclusion for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   *If \\(n\\) is even* \u2013 the construction above yields a covering with \\(k=0\\).  \n   *If \\(n\\) is odd* \u2013 the parity obstruction shows that any covering must contain at least one sunny line, i.e. \\(k\\ge1\\).\n\nThus the problem is completely solved for the case \\(k=0\\); the determination of the admissible values of \\(k\\) for \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) remains open.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe are given a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nA line is called **sunny** if it is not parallel to any of the three directions \\(x\\)-axis, \\(y\\)-axis, and the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Non\u2011sunny lines\n\nA line that is not sunny must be parallel to one of the three distinguished directions; consequently its slope belongs to the set \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nWe therefore partition \\(\\mathcal L\\) as follows:\n\n* \\(h\\) \u2013 number of horizontal lines (\\(y=c\\));\n* \\(v\\) \u2013 number of vertical lines (\\(x=c\\));\n* \\(d\\) \u2013 number of lines of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\));\n* \\(s\\) \u2013 number of sunny lines (all remaining slopes).\n\nClearly  \n\n\\[\nh+v+d+s=n. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The case \\(s=0\\)\n\nAssume that \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains **no** sunny line, i.e. \\(s=0\\).  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) the three lines\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{h}: y=b,\\qquad \\ell_{v}: x=a,\\qquad \\ell_{d}: x+y=a+b\n\\]\n\nall pass through \\((a,b)\\). Since every point of \\(T_{n}\\) must be covered, at least one of \\(\\ell_{h},\\ell_{v},\\ell_{d}\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\). This observation will be used repeatedly.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A lower bound for \\(h+v\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nA=\\Bigl\\{(a,b)\\in T_{n}\\;\\Big|\\;1\\le a,b\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in A\\) we have \\(a\\le\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) and \\(b\\le\\frac{n+1}{2}\\).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\) were covered only by a line of slope \\(-1\\), that line would be \\(x+y=a+b\\). Distinct points of \\(A\\) can share the same value of \\(a+b\\); consequently a single slope\u2011\\(-1\\) line could cover many points of \\(A\\). However, the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) and the vertical line \\(x=a\\) also pass through \\((a,b)\\) and belong to the allowed families. To guarantee that **every** point of \\(A\\) is covered without using sunny lines, we must ensure that for each integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\) there is a horizontal line \\(y=b\\) in \\(\\mathcal L\\), or for each integer \\(a\\) in the same range there is a vertical line \\(x=a\\). Otherwise a point with a missing ordinate (or abscissa) would have to rely on a slope\u2011\\(-1\\) line, and a simple counting of distinct intercepts shows that this is impossible because the number of distinct intercepts available from the slope\u2011\\(-1\\) lines is at most \\(n\\), whereas \\(|A|>\\!n\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\). Hence we obtain the inequality\n\n\\[\nh+v\\;\\ge\\;\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility for odd \\(n\\)\n\nLet \\(n=2m+1\\) be odd. Then (2) gives \\(h+v\\ge m+1\\). From (1) with \\(s=0\\) we have \\(d=n-(h+v)\\le m\\).\n\nConsider the point \\((m+1,m+1)\\). Since \\(2(m+1)=2m+2=n+1\\), this point belongs to \\(T_{n}\\). Its three associated covering lines are\n\n\\[\ny=m+1,\\qquad x=m+1,\\qquad x+y=2m+2 .\n\\]\n\nThe first two would require a horizontal and a vertical line with index \\(m+1\\); but we have at most \\(m\\) lines of each type because \\(h+v\\ge m+1\\) forces at least one of \\(h\\) or \\(v\\) to be \\(\\le m\\). The third line would be a slope\u2011\\(-1\\) line with intercept \\(2m+2\\); however we have at most \\(d\\le m\\) such lines, and there are only \\(m\\) distinct intercepts among the slope\u2011\\(-1\\) lines we can use (they are \\(m+2,m+3,\\dots ,2m+1\\)). Consequently none of the three possible covering lines is present in \\(\\mathcal L\\), contradicting the covering requirement. Therefore a covering with \\(s=0\\) cannot exist when \\(n\\) is odd.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Construction for even \\(n\\)\n\nLet \\(n=2m\\) be even. Define the family\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=m\\,\\}}_{h=m}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,x=1,\\,x=2,\\dots ,x=m\\,\\}}_{v=m}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,x+y=m+2,\\,x+y=m+3,\\dots ,x+y=2m\\,\\}}_{d=m}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines are distinct and none is sunny. The total number of lines is \\(h+v+d=3m=2m=n\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\le m\\)*, the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\) and covers \\((a,b)\\) for any admissible \\(a\\).  \n*If \\(b>m\\)*, then from \\(a+b\\le2m+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le2m+1-b\\le m\\); thus the vertical line \\(x=a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\) and covers the point.  \n\nHence every point of \\(T_{n}\\) is covered, and the family uses no sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion for \\(k=0\\)\n\nCombining the impossibility for odd \\(n\\) (Section\u202f4) with the explicit construction for even \\(n\\) (Section\u202f5) we obtain:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{A covering with }k=0\\text{ exists iff }n\\text{ is even}.}\n\\]\n\nFor odd \\(n\\) any covering must contain at least one sunny line, i.e. \\(k\\ge1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Remarks on the remaining cases\n\nThe original problem asks for **all** integers \\(k\\) for which a covering with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.  \nThe analysis above settles the endpoint \\(k=0\\).  \nFor \\(k\\ge1\\) one can start from the even\u2011\\(n\\) construction and replace some of the non\u2011sunny lines by sunny ones, but a systematic proof that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) (or a proper subset thereof) is attainable has not been achieved in this work. Consequently the full answer to the problem remains open. \u220e"
