            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nThe only admissible value of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition, and **all** of those lines must be non‑sunny (i.e. each line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)). No configuration with even a single sunny line can meet the requirements.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   The set \\(T_n\\) consists of the lattice points that have to lie on at least one of the chosen lines.\n\n2. **Existence for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n   \\]\n   are pairwise distinct, each is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (hence not sunny), and every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\). Thus the covering condition is fulfilled with \\(k=0\\).\n\n3. **Impossibility of any sunny line.**  \n   Assume, for contradiction, that a family \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains at least one sunny line.  \n   *A sunny line* is a line whose slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) consider the **column**\n   \\[\n   C_c=\\{(c,b)\\mid b\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\}.\n   \\]\n   The column \\(C_c\\) contains exactly \\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   - A vertical line can contain **all** points of a column.  \n   - Any non‑vertical line (in particular any sunny line) meets a given column in **at most one** point, because two distinct points of the same column would have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate and therefore would force the line to be vertical.  \n   - A horizontal line meets a column in at most one point as well (its \\(y\\)-coordinate is fixed).\n\n   Let \\(v\\) be the number of vertical lines in \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) and let \\(s\\) be the number of sunny lines (so \\(s\\ge 1\\) by assumption). The remaining lines, if any, are horizontal; denote their number by \\(h\\). Thus\n   \\[\n   v+h+s=n. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   For a fixed column \\(C_c\\) the points of \\(C_c\\) can be covered only by:\n   * the vertical line \\(x=c\\) (if it belongs to \\(\\mathcal{F}\\));\n   * at most one point contributed by each non‑vertical line (sunny or horizontal).\n\n   Consequently the total number of points that can be covered in column \\(C_c\\) is at most\n   \\[\n   \\mathbf{1}_{\\{x=c\\in\\mathcal{F}\\}}+ (n-v) ,\n   \\]\n   because there are exactly \\(n-v\\) non‑vertical lines (the \\(s\\) sunny lines together with the \\(h\\) horizontal lines). Since \\(\\mathbf{1}_{\\{x=c\\in\\mathcal{F}\\}}\\le 1\\), the maximal possible number of points covered in any column is\n   \\[\n   1+(n-v). \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   However the column \\(C_1\\) contains \\(n\\) points (all points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\)). To cover these \\(n\\) points we must have\n   \\[\n   1+(n-v)\\ge n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad v\\le 1. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Similarly, the column \\(C_2\\) contains \\(n-1\\) points, which forces\n   \\[\n   1+(n-v)\\ge n-1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad v\\le 2. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Continuing this argument for the column \\(C_{n-1}\\), which contains \\(2\\) points, yields\n   \\[\n   1+(n-v)\\ge 2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad v\\ge n-1. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Inequalities (3) and (5) together give \\(v\\le 1\\) and \\(v\\ge n-1\\). Because \\(n\\ge 3\\), these two bounds are incompatible. Hence our assumption that a sunny line exists leads to a contradiction.\n\n   Therefore **no** sunny line can appear in a family of \\(n\\) lines satisfying the covering condition. All lines must be non‑sunny, i.e. each line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The covering condition can be fulfilled with \\(n\\) lines, but every admissible family necessarily contains zero sunny lines. Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\text{ is the only possible value.}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
