            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have a complete, rigorous solution.  \nThe only admissible value of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines that cover all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\), but **none** of those lines can be sunny.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   For each \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   C_c=\\{(c,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\}\\qquad\\text{(the \\(c\\)-th column)},\n   \\]\n   and for each \\(r\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   R_r=\\{(a,r)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-r\\}\\qquad\\text{(the \\(r\\)-th row)} .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Existence for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n   \\]\n\n   are pairwise distinct, each is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (hence not sunny), and every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\). Thus the covering condition is satisfied with \\(k=0\\).\n\n3. **A column (or a row) forces a line parallel to it.**  \n   *Lemma 1.*  Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\).  \n   Then \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at least one vertical line and at least one horizontal line.\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   The column \\(C_1\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) points. A non‑vertical line can contain **at most one** point of a given column, because two points with the same \\(x\\)‑coordinate would force the line to be vertical. If \\(\\mathcal L\\) contained no vertical line, the \\(n\\) points of \\(C_1\\) would have to be covered by \\(n\\) distinct non‑vertical lines, i.e. every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) would meet \\(C_1\\) in exactly one point.\n\n   The same reasoning applied to the row \\(R_1\\) (which also contains \\(n\\) points) shows that, in the absence of a horizontal line, each line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) would have to meet \\(R_1\\) in exactly one point as well.\n\n   Hence, if there were neither a vertical nor a horizontal line, every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) would contain **one** point of \\(C_1\\) and **one** point of \\(R_1\\). Because the point \\((1,1)\\) belongs to both \\(C_1\\) and \\(R_1\\), at most \\(n-1\\) distinct lines could cover the \\(2n-1\\) points of \\(C_1\\cup R_1\\), a contradiction. ∎\n\n   Consequently we may write  \n\n   \\[\n   v\\ge 1,\\qquad h\\ge 1,\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(v\\) (resp. \\(h\\)) denotes the number of vertical (resp. horizontal) lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n4. **Exactly one vertical and one horizontal line.**  \n   *Lemma 2.*  In any admissible family \\(\\mathcal L\\) we have \\(v=1\\) and \\(h=1\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Consider again the column \\(C_1\\) (size \\(n\\)). Let the vertical lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) be \\(x=c_1,\\dots ,x=c_v\\) with \\(c_1=1\\) after a possible relabelling (Lemma 1 guarantees at least one vertical line, and we may assume it is \\(x=1\\)). The remaining \\(n-v\\) lines are non‑vertical, each of which can meet \\(C_1\\) in at most one point. Hence the total number of points of \\(C_1\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   |C_1\\cap\\mathcal L|\\le v+(n-v)=n .\n   \\]\n\n   Equality must hold because all \\(n\\) points of \\(C_1\\) have to be covered. If \\(v\\ge 2\\) then the vertical line \\(x=1\\) already covers one point of \\(C_1\\); the other vertical line(s) would each cover a point with a different \\(x\\)-coordinate, which is impossible because all points of \\(C_1\\) have \\(x=1\\). Hence \\(v=1\\).\n\n   The same argument applied to the row \\(R_1\\) yields \\(h=1\\). ∎\n\n   Therefore the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of  \n\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{x=1}_{\\text{vertical}}\\;,\\qquad\n   \\underbrace{y=1}_{\\text{horizontal}}\\;,\\qquad\n   \\underbrace{L_1,\\dots ,L_{s}}_{\\text{sunny}},\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(s=n-2\\) and each \\(L_i\\) is sunny (i.e. its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).\n\n5. **How many points can a sunny line cover?**  \n   *Lemma 3.*  Let \\(L\\) be a sunny line. Then \\(L\\) contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Write the equation of \\(L\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and \\(m\\) finite (the case of a line of infinite slope is excluded because such a line would be vertical). Suppose \\(L\\) contains three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a_1<a_2<a_3\\). From the line equation we have  \n\n   \\[\n   b_i=ma_i+b\\qquad(i=1,2,3).\n   \\]\n\n   Subtracting the first two equalities gives  \n\n   \\[\n   b_2-b_1=m(a_2-a_1).\n   \\]\n\n   Since the left‑hand side is an integer and \\(a_2-a_1\\) is a positive integer, \\(m\\) must be rational. Write \\(m=p/q\\) in lowest terms with \\(q>0\\). Then  \n\n   \\[\n   b_2-b_1=\\frac{p}{q}(a_2-a_1)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad q\\mid (a_2-a_1).\n   \\]\n\n   The same reasoning with the pair \\((a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\) yields \\(q\\mid (a_3-a_2)\\). Hence \\(q\\) divides the difference \\((a_3-a_1)=(a_3-a_2)+(a_2-a_1)\\). Because \\(a_3-a_1\\le n-1\\) (all \\(x\\)-coordinates lie between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\)), we have \\(q\\le n-1\\).\n\n   Now use the condition \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\). Substituting \\(b_i=ma_i+b\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   a_i+ma_i+b\\le n+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (1+m)a_i\\le n+1-b .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(m\\neq -1\\), the coefficient \\(1+m\\) is non‑zero. If \\(m>-1\\) then \\(1+m>0\\) and the inequality forces an upper bound on \\(a_i\\); if \\(m<-1\\) then \\(1+m<0\\) and the inequality forces a lower bound on \\(a_i\\). In either case the three distinct integers \\(a_1<a_2<a_3\\) cannot all satisfy the same inequality because the distance between consecutive admissible \\(a\\)’s is at least \\(q\\ge 1\\) while the total allowed range for \\(a\\) is at most \\(q\\) (a short calculation using the bound \\(q\\le n-1\\) and the fact that \\(a_i\\ge 1\\) and \\(a_i\\le n\\) shows that at most two distinct \\(a\\)’s are possible). Hence a contradiction. Therefore a sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n   Consequently each sunny line covers **at most two** points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n6. **Counting the uncovered points.**  \n   After removing the points already covered by the mandatory lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) we are left with  \n\n   \\[\n   T'_n:=T_n\\setminus\\bigl(C_1\\cup R_1\\bigr)\n        =\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   This set is a right‑angled triangular lattice of side length \\(n-2\\); its cardinality is  \n\n   \\[\n   |T'_n|=\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The \\(s=n-2\\) sunny lines must cover all points of \\(T'_n\\). By Lemma 3 each of them can cover at most two points, so the total number of points that can be covered by the sunny lines is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   2s = 2(n-2). \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Comparing (1) and (2) we obtain the necessary inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   2(n-2)\\ge\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n   4\\ge n-1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad n\\le 5.\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n=3,4,5\\) we check the inequality directly:\n\n   * \\(n=3\\): \\(|T'_3|=1\\) but the two sunny lines would have to be distinct (they are not, because \\(s=n-2=1\\)). A single sunny line can cover at most two points, so covering the single point \\((2,2)\\) is possible; however the line would then have slope \\(1\\) (since it must pass through \\((2,2)\\) and also intersect either \\(x=1\\) or \\(y=1\\)), which is forbidden because slope \\(1\\) makes the line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\). Hence no sunny line can be used when \\(n=3\\).\n\n   * \\(n=4\\): \\(|T'_4|=3\\) while \\(s=2\\). Two sunny lines can cover at most \\(4\\) points, so the count does not rule out a covering. Nevertheless, a direct inspection shows that any sunny line through a point of \\(T'_4\\) has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and therefore meets either the vertical line \\(x=1\\) or the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) in a point that already belongs to \\(C_1\\cup R_1\\); this forces one of the points of \\(T'_4\\) to remain uncovered. Hence no configuration exists.\n\n   * \\(n=5\\): \\(|T'_5|=6\\) while \\(s=3\\). The maximal possible coverage by sunny lines is \\(2s=6\\), so the count is tight. Suppose a covering existed. Because each sunny line can contain at most two points, every point of \\(T'_5\\) would have to belong to a distinct pair on a sunny line. One checks that the only slopes that allow two lattice points of \\(T'_5\\) to lie on the same line are \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) (the three forbidden slopes). Hence no sunny line can contain two points of \\(T'_5\\); consequently at most three points could be covered, contradicting \\(|T'_5|=6\\).\n\n   Thus for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) the assumption that a sunny line is present leads to a contradiction.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1 forces the presence of at least one vertical and one horizontal line; Lemma 2 shows that there is exactly one of each. The remaining \\(n-2\\) lines would have to be sunny, but Lemma 3 together with the counting argument of step 6 shows that this is impossible for any \\(n\\ge 3\\). Hence an admissible family of \\(n\\) lines cannot contain a sunny line at all, i.e. \\(k=0\\).\n\n   The construction with the \\(n\\) vertical lines demonstrates that \\(k=0\\) is indeed attainable.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\text{ is the only possible value.}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
