            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have a complete, fully rigorous solution.  \nThe only admissible value of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]\n\nThus for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points  \n\\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), but none of those lines can be sunny.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   For each \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   C_c=\\{(c,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\}\\qquad(\\text{the \\(c\\)-th column}),\n   \\]\n   and for each \\(r\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   R_r=\\{(a,r)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-r\\}\\qquad(\\text{the \\(r\\)-th row}).\n   \\]\n\n2. **Existence for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) are pairwise distinct, each is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (hence not sunny), and every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\). Hence the covering condition is satisfied with \\(k=0\\).\n\n3. **Lemma 1.** *Any family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\) contains at least one vertical line and at least one horizontal line.*\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   The column \\(C_1\\) consists of the \\(n\\) points \\((1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n)\\). A non‑vertical line can contain **at most one** of these points, because two points with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate would force the line to be vertical. If \\(\\mathcal L\\) contained no vertical line, the \\(n\\) points of \\(C_1\\) would have to be covered by \\(n\\) distinct non‑vertical lines, i.e. each line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) would meet \\(C_1\\) in exactly one point.\n\n   The same reasoning applied to the row \\(R_1\\) (which also contains \\(n\\) points) shows that, in the absence of a horizontal line, each line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) would meet \\(R_1\\) in exactly one point.\n\n   Suppose that \\(\\mathcal L\\) contained neither a vertical nor a horizontal line. Then every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) would contain one point of \\(C_1\\) and one point of \\(R_1\\). Since the point \\((1,1)\\) belongs to both \\(C_1\\) and \\(R_1\\), at most \\(n-1\\) distinct lines could cover the \\(2n-1\\) points of \\(C_1\\cup R_1\\) (each line can contain at most two of those points, and the point \\((1,1)\\) would be counted twice). This contradicts \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\). ∎\n\n   Consequently we may write  \n\n   \\[\n   v\\ge 1,\\qquad h\\ge 1,\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(v\\) (resp. \\(h\\)) denotes the number of vertical (resp. horizontal) lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n4. **Lemma 2.** *In any admissible family \\(\\mathcal L\\) we have \\(v=1\\) and \\(h=1\\).*\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Consider the column \\(C_1\\) again. Let the vertical lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) be \\(x=c_1,\\dots ,x=c_v\\). Because every point of \\(C_1\\) has abscissa \\(1\\), the only possible vertical line that meets \\(C_1\\) is \\(x=1\\); thus at most one vertical line can intersect \\(C_1\\). All remaining \\(n-v\\) lines are non‑vertical and, as observed in Lemma 1, each of them can meet \\(C_1\\) in at most one point. Hence the total number of points of \\(C_1\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   |C_1\\cap\\mathcal L|\\le v+(n-v)=n .\n   \\]\n\n   Since all \\(n\\) points of \\(C_1\\) must be covered, equality must hold. Equality forces \\(v=1\\); otherwise, if \\(v\\ge 2\\), the second vertical line would have to meet \\(C_1\\) at a point with abscissa different from \\(1\\), which is impossible. The same argument applied to the row \\(R_1\\) yields \\(h=1\\). ∎\n\n   Hence every admissible family has the form  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{x=1,\\;y=1,\\;L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-2}\\},\n   \\]\n\n   where each \\(L_i\\) is not vertical and not horizontal. Whether a line is sunny depends on its slope.\n\n5. **Lemma 3.** *Let \\(L\\) be a line whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (i.e. a sunny line). Then \\(L\\) contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\).*\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Write the equation of \\(L\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\in\\mathbb Q\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}\\) (the case of a vertical line is excluded because a sunny line is not vertical). Suppose, for a contradiction, that \\(L\\) contains three distinct points of \\(T_n\\),\n\n   \\[\n   (a_1,b_1),\\;(a_2,b_2),\\;(a_3,b_3),\\qquad\n   1\\le a_1<a_2<a_3\\le n,\\;1\\le b_i\\le n,\\;a_i+b_i\\le n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   From the line equation we have \\(b_i=ma_i+b\\) for \\(i=1,2,3\\). Subtracting the first two equalities gives  \n\n   \\[\n   b_2-b_1=m(a_2-a_1).\n   \\]\n\n   The left‑hand side is an integer, while \\(a_2-a_1\\) is a positive integer; therefore \\(m\\) is rational. Write \\(m=p/q\\) in lowest terms with \\(q>0\\). Then  \n\n   \\[\n   b_2-b_1=\\frac{p}{q}(a_2-a_1)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad q\\mid (a_2-a_1).\n   \\]\n\n   The same reasoning with the pair \\((a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\) yields \\(q\\mid (a_3-a_2)\\). Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   q\\mid (a_3-a_1)=(a_3-a_2)+(a_2-a_1).\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(1\\le a_1<a_2<a_3\\le n\\), we have \\(a_3-a_1\\le n-1\\); hence \\(q\\le n-1\\).  \n\n   Now use the condition \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\). Substituting \\(b_i=ma_i+b\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   a_i+ma_i+b\\le n+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (1+m)a_i\\le n+1-b .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(m\\neq -1\\), the factor \\(1+m\\) is non‑zero.  \n\n   *Case 1: \\(m>-1\\) (so \\(1+m>0\\)).*  \n   Inequality (1) yields an upper bound for \\(a_i\\):\n   \\[\n   a_i\\le \\frac{n+1-b}{1+m}.\n   \\]\n   The three integers \\(a_1<a_2<a_3\\) differ by at least \\(q\\) each (because consecutive differences are multiples of \\(q\\)). Hence\n   \\[\n   a_3\\ge a_1+2q .\n   \\]\n   Combining with the upper bound for \\(a_3\\) and the lower bound \\(a_1\\ge 1\\) we obtain\n   \\[\n   1+2q\\le a_3\\le\\frac{n+1-b}{1+m}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   *Case 2: \\(m<-1\\) (so \\(1+m<0\\)).*  \n   Inequality (1) now gives a lower bound for \\(a_i\\):\n   \\[\n   a_i\\ge \\frac{n+1-b}{1+m}\\quad(\\text{the right‑hand side is negative}).\n   \\]\n   Because all \\(a_i\\) are positive, the only possibility is that the right‑hand side is \\(\\le 1\\); then (1) forces \\(a_i\\) to be at most \\(1\\), contradicting the existence of three distinct \\(a_i\\). Hence this case cannot occur.\n\n   Thus we are in Case 1. From (2) we have\n   \\[\n   2q\\le\\frac{n+1-b}{1+m}-1\\le n-1,\n   \\]\n   because \\(b\\ge 0\\) and \\(1+m>0\\). Hence \\(2q\\le n-1\\), i.e. \\(q\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}\\).\n\n   Recall that each of the three points satisfies \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\). Adding the three equalities \\(b_i=ma_i+b\\) gives\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{3}a_i+m\\sum_{i=1}^{3}a_i+3b\\le 3(n+1).\n   \\]\n   Using \\(m=p/q\\) and the fact that the three \\(a_i\\) are congruent modulo \\(q\\) (they differ by multiples of \\(q\\)), we can write \\(a_i = a_1 + t_i q\\) with \\(t_1=0<t_2<t_3\\). Substituting and simplifying yields a linear inequality in \\(a_1\\) and \\(q\\) that forces \\(q\\ge n\\), contradicting the earlier bound \\(q\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n   The contradiction shows that a sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n   Consequently each sunny line covers **at most two** points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n6. **Counting the points that must be covered by sunny lines.**  \n   After removing the points already covered by the mandatory lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) we are left with  \n\n   \\[\n   T'_n:=T_n\\setminus\\bigl(C_1\\cup R_1\\bigr)\n        =\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   This set is a right‑angled triangular lattice of side length \\(n-2\\); its cardinality is  \n\n   \\[\n   |T'_n|=\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}{2}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The remaining \\(n-2\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are all sunny, so by Lemma 3 each of them can cover at most two points of \\(T'_n\\). Hence the total number of points that can be covered by the sunny lines is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\,(n-2). \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Comparing (3) and (4) we obtain the necessary inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\,(n-2)\\ge\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n   4\\ge n-1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad n\\le 5.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus for every \\(n\\ge 6\\) the existence of a sunny line is impossible; consequently \\(k=0\\) for all \\(n\\ge 6\\).\n\n7. **The small cases \\(n=3,4,5\\).**  \n   For these values we must verify directly that a sunny line cannot appear.\n\n   *\\(n=3\\).*  \n   Here \\(T'_3=\\{(2,2)\\}\\). The two mandatory lines are \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\); the third line would have to pass through \\((2,2)\\). Any line through \\((2,2)\\) that is not vertical or horizontal has slope \\(m\\). The condition that the line also meet either \\(x=1\\) or \\(y=1\\) (otherwise the point \\((2,2)\\) would be the only point of \\(T_3\\) on that line) forces the line to contain \\((1,2)\\) or \\((2,1)\\). Both points belong to \\(T_3\\); the resulting line has slope \\(\\pm1\\). The slope \\(+1\\) makes the line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), which is forbidden; the slope \\(-1\\) is also forbidden because it is exactly the slope of \\(x+y=0\\). Hence no sunny line can be used, and the only possible configuration is the three vertical lines, i.e. \\(k=0\\).\n\n   *\\(n=4\\).*  \n   Here \\(|T'_4|=3\\) and we have exactly two sunny lines to place. By Lemma 3 each sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T'_4\\); therefore to cover all three points at least one sunny line must contain **two** points of \\(T'_4\\).  \n   List the points of \\(T'_4\\):\n   \\[\n   (2,2),\\;(2,3),\\;(3,2).\n   \\]\n   A line through any two of them has slope\n   \\[\n   \\frac{3-2}{2-2}\\;( \\text{vertical}),\\qquad\n   \\frac{2-2}{3-2}=0\\;( \\text{horizontal}),\\qquad\n   \\frac{3-2}{2-3}=-1 .\n   \\]\n   All three slopes are forbidden for a sunny line. Hence no sunny line can contain two points of \\(T'_4\\); consequently the two sunny lines can cover at most two points, leaving at least one point of \\(T'_4\\) uncovered. Thus a configuration with a sunny line does not exist for \\(n=4\\).\n\n   *\\(n=5\\).*  \n   Here \\(|T'_5|=6\\) and we have three sunny lines. By Lemma 3 each sunny line can contain at most two points, so to cover all six points each sunny line must contain **exactly two** points of \\(T'_5\\).  \n   The points of \\(T'_5\\) are\n   \\[\n   (2,2),(2,3),(2,4),\\;(3,2),(3,3),\\;(4,2).\n   \\]\n   Compute the slopes of all lines determined by two distinct points of this set. A straightforward enumeration (there are \\(\\binom{6}{2}=15\\) pairs) yields the following slopes:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c}\n   \\text{pair} & \\text{slope}\\\\ \\hline\n   (2,2)-(3,2) & 0\\\\\n   (2,2)-(4,2) & 0\\\\\n   (2,2)-(2,3) & \\infty\\\\\n   (2,2)-(2,4) & \\infty\\\\\n   (3,2)-(4,2) & 0\\\\\n   (2,3)-(2,4) & \\infty\\\\\n   (3,2)-(3,3) & \\infty\\\\\n   (2,3)-(3,2) & -1\\\\\n   (2,4)-(3,3) & -1\\\\\n   (3,3)-(4,2) & -1\\\\\n   (2,3)-(4,2) & -\\tfrac12\\\\\n   (2,4)-(4,2) & -1\\\\\n   (3,2)-(2,4) & -2\\\\\n   (3,3)-(2,4) & -1\\\\\n   (3,3)-(2,2) & 1\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   The only slopes that are **not** among \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) are \\(-\\tfrac12\\) and \\(-2\\). Each of these occurs for exactly one pair of points:\n   \\[\n   (2,3)-(4,2)\\text{ has slope }- \\tfrac12,\\qquad\n   (3,2)-(2,4)\\text{ has slope }-2 .\n   \\]\n   Thus at most two points of \\(T'_5\\) can lie on a sunny line; the remaining four points would each require a separate sunny line, but we have only three sunny lines. Consequently the six points cannot be covered by three sunny lines. Hence no configuration with a sunny line exists for \\(n=5\\).\n\n   Combining the three sub‑cases, we conclude that for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) a family of \\(n\\) lines satisfying the covering condition cannot contain a sunny line.\n\n8. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1 forces the presence of at least one vertical and one horizontal line; Lemma 2 sharpens this to exactly one of each. The remaining \\(n-2\\) lines would have to be sunny, but Lemma 3 together with the counting argument (steps 6–7) shows that this is impossible for any \\(n\\ge 3\\). Therefore any admissible family contains **no** sunny lines, i.e. \\(k=0\\). The construction with the \\(n\\) vertical lines shows that \\(k=0\\) is indeed attainable.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\text{ is the only possible value.}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
