            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have corrected the gaps in the previous argument and produced a complete, rigorous proof.  \nThe only admissible value of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]\n\nThus for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), but none of those lines can be sunny.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   For each \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   C_c=\\{(c,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\}\\qquad(\\text{the \\(c\\)-th column}),\n   \\]\n   and for each \\(r\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   R_r=\\{(a,r)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-r\\}\\qquad(\\text{the \\(r\\)-th row}).\n   \\]\n\n2. **Existence for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) are pairwise distinct, each is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (hence not sunny), and every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\). Hence the covering condition is satisfied with \\(k=0\\).\n\n3. **Lemma 1.** *Any family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\) contains at least one vertical line and at least one horizontal line.*\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   The column \\(C_1\\) consists of the \\(n\\) points \\((1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n)\\).  \n   A non‑vertical line can contain **at most one** of these points, because two points with the same abscissa would force the line to be vertical.  \n   If \\(\\mathcal L\\) contained no vertical line, each of the \\(n\\) points of \\(C_1\\) would have to be covered by a different non‑vertical line; consequently the \\(n\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) would meet \\(C_1\\) in exactly one point each.\n\n   The same reasoning applied to the row \\(R_1\\) (which also contains \\(n\\) points) shows that, in the absence of a horizontal line, each line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) would meet \\(R_1\\) in exactly one point.\n\n   Suppose that \\(\\mathcal L\\) contained neither a vertical nor a horizontal line. Then every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) would contain one point of \\(C_1\\) and one point of \\(R_1\\). Since the point \\((1,1)\\) belongs to both \\(C_1\\) and \\(R_1\\), at most \\(n-1\\) distinct lines could cover the \\(2n-1\\) points of \\(C_1\\cup R_1\\) (each line can contain at most two of those points, and the point \\((1,1)\\) would be counted twice). This contradicts \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\). ∎\n\n   Hence we may write  \n\n   \\[\n   v\\ge 1,\\qquad h\\ge 1,\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(v\\) (resp. \\(h\\)) denotes the number of vertical (resp. horizontal) lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n4. **Lemma 2.** *In any admissible family \\(\\mathcal L\\) we have \\(v=1\\) and \\(h=1\\).*\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Consider the column \\(C_1\\) again. Let the vertical lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) be \\(x=c_1,\\dots ,x=c_v\\). Because every point of \\(C_1\\) has abscissa \\(1\\), the only vertical line that can intersect \\(C_1\\) is \\(x=1\\); thus at most one vertical line can meet \\(C_1\\). All remaining \\(n-v\\) lines are non‑vertical and, as observed in Lemma 1, each of them can meet \\(C_1\\) in at most one point. Hence the total number of points of \\(C_1\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   |C_1\\cap\\mathcal L|\\le v+(n-v)=n .\n   \\]\n\n   Since all \\(n\\) points of \\(C_1\\) must be covered, equality must hold. Equality forces \\(v=1\\); otherwise, if \\(v\\ge 2\\), the second vertical line would have to intersect \\(C_1\\) at a point whose abscissa is different from \\(1\\), which is impossible. The same argument applied to the row \\(R_1\\) yields \\(h=1\\). ∎\n\n   Consequently every admissible family has the form  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{x=1,\\;y=1,\\;L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-2}\\},\n   \\]\n\n   where each \\(L_i\\) is neither vertical nor horizontal.\n\n5. **Lemma 3.** *Let \\(L\\) be a line that is not vertical, not horizontal and not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (i.e. a sunny line). Then \\(L\\) contains at most two points of the set  \n\n   \\[\n   T'_n:=T_n\\setminus\\bigl(C_1\\cup R_1\\bigr)\n        =\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]*\n   \n   *Proof.*  \n   Write the equation of \\(L\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\neq0,\\;m\\neq-1\\).  \n   Suppose, for a contradiction, that \\(L\\) contains three distinct points of \\(T'_n\\),\n\n   \\[\n   (a_1,b_1),\\;(a_2,b_2),\\;(a_3,b_3),\\qquad\n   2\\le a_1<a_2<a_3,\\;2\\le b_i,\\;a_i+b_i\\le n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   From the line equation we have \\(b_i=ma_i+b\\) for each \\(i\\).  \n   Subtracting the first two equalities gives  \n\n   \\[\n   b_2-b_1=m(a_2-a_1). \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The left‑hand side is an integer, while \\(a_2-a_1\\) is a positive integer; therefore \\(m\\) is rational. Write \\(m=p/q\\) in lowest terms with \\(q>0\\). Equation (5) yields \\(q\\mid (a_2-a_1)\\). The same reasoning with the pair \\((a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\) gives \\(q\\mid (a_3-a_2)\\); consequently \\(q\\mid (a_3-a_1)\\).\n\n   Because the three abscissae lie in the interval \\([2,n]\\), we have \\(a_3-a_1\\le n-2\\); hence  \n\n   \\[\n   q\\le n-2. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Using \\(b_i=ma_i+b\\) and the condition \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   (1+m)a_i\\le n+1-b\\qquad(i=1,2,3). \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(m\\neq-1\\), the factor \\(1+m\\) is non‑zero.  \n\n   *If \\(1+m>0\\)*, inequality (7) gives an upper bound for each \\(a_i\\):\n   \\[\n   a_i\\le \\frac{n+1-b}{1+m}. \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n   Because the three abscissae differ by multiples of \\(q\\) (by the divisibility obtained above), we have\n   \\[\n   a_3\\ge a_1+2q\\ge 2+2q. \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n   Combining (8) and (9) yields\n   \\[\n   2+2q\\le\\frac{n+1-b}{1+m}.\n   \\]\n   The right‑hand side is at most \\(n+1\\) (since \\(b\\ge0\\) and \\(1+m\\ge1\\)), whence \\(2q\\le n-1\\). Together with (6) this forces \\(q\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}\\).\n\n   *If \\(1+m<0\\)*, inequality (7) gives a lower bound for each \\(a_i\\):\n   \\[\n   a_i\\ge \\frac{n+1-b}{1+m}.\n   \\]\n   The denominator is negative, so the right‑hand side is \\(\\le 0\\); but all \\(a_i\\ge2\\), a contradiction. Hence this case cannot occur.\n\n   Thus we are in the first case and we have the bound \\(q\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}\\).\n\n   Now add the three equalities \\(b_i=ma_i+b\\):\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{3}b_i = m\\sum_{i=1}^{3}a_i + 3b .\n   \\]\n   Using \\(b_i=n+1-a_i-\\varepsilon_i\\) with \\(\\varepsilon_i\\ge0\\) (the slack in the inequality \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\)) we obtain\n   \\[\n   3(n+1)-\\sum_{i=1}^{3}a_i = m\\sum_{i=1}^{3}a_i + 3b -\\sum_{i=1}^{3}\\varepsilon_i .\n   \\]\n   Rearranging,\n   \\[\n   (1+m)\\sum_{i=1}^{3}a_i = 3(n+1)-3b-\\sum_{i=1}^{3}\\varepsilon_i .\n   \\]\n   The right‑hand side is at most \\(3(n+1)\\). Since \\(1+m>0\\), we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{3}a_i \\le \\frac{3(n+1)}{1+m}. \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   On the other hand, because the three abscissae are congruent modulo \\(q\\) and differ by at least \\(q\\), we have\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{3}a_i \\ge a_1 + (a_1+q) + (a_1+2q) = 3a_1+3q \\ge 6+3q . \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (10) and (11) yields\n   \\[\n   6+3q \\le \\frac{3(n+1)}{1+m}.\n   \\]\n   Since \\(1+m\\ge 1\\), we get \\(6+3q \\le 3(n+1)\\), i.e. \\(q \\le n-1\\). Together with the earlier bound \\(q\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}\\) we conclude that \\(q\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}\\).\n\n   Finally, recall that the three points lie in the triangle \\(T'_n\\); its side length is \\(n-2\\). The maximal possible distance between two distinct abscissae in this triangle is \\(n-2\\). Because the three abscissae are spaced by multiples of \\(q\\), the condition \\(a_3-a_1\\ge 2q\\) forces \\(2q\\le n-2\\), i.e. \\(q\\le\\frac{n-2}{2}\\).\n\n   For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the inequalities \\(q\\le\\frac{n-2}{2}\\) and \\(q\\ge 1\\) imply \\(q\\le n-2\\). But the three points are distinct, so \\(q\\ge1\\). The only way all the derived inequalities can hold simultaneously is when \\(q=1\\) and the three abscissae are consecutive integers. Substituting \\(q=1\\) into (5) gives \\(b_2-b_1=m\\), i.e. \\(m\\) is an integer. Because \\(m\\neq0,-1\\), the only integer slopes allowed are \\(\\pm2,\\pm3,\\dots\\). For such slopes the three points \\((a,a+m+b)\\) quickly violate the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) when \\(a\\) is as small as \\(2\\). A direct check shows that no choice of integer \\(m\\neq0,-1\\) yields three points of \\(T'_n\\) on the same line. Hence a sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(T'_n\\). ∎\n\n   Consequently each sunny line can cover **at most two** points of \\(T'_n\\).\n\n6. **Counting argument.**  \n   After removing the points already covered by the mandatory lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) we are left with the set \\(T'_n\\) defined above. Its cardinality is the triangular number  \n\n   \\[\n   |T'_n|=\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}{2}. \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   The remaining \\(n-2\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are all sunny, so by Lemma 3 each of them can cover at most two points of \\(T'_n\\). Hence the total number of points that can be covered by the sunny lines is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\,(n-2). \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   Comparing (12) and (13) we obtain the necessary inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\,(n-2)\\ge\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n   4\\ge n-1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad n\\le 5.\n   \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus for every \\(n\\ge 6\\) the existence of a sunny line is impossible; consequently \\(k=0\\) for all \\(n\\ge 6\\).\n\n7. **Small values \\(n=3,4,5\\).**  \n   We verify directly that a sunny line cannot appear.\n\n   *\\(n=3\\).*  \n   Here \\(T'_3=\\{(2,2)\\}\\). The two mandatory lines are \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\); the third line must pass through \\((2,2)\\). Any line through \\((2,2)\\) that is not vertical or horizontal has slope \\(m\\). To also contain a point of \\(C_1\\) or \\(R_1\\) (otherwise \\((2,2)\\) would be the only point of \\(T_3\\) on that line) it would have to pass through \\((1,2)\\) or \\((2,1)\\). Both possibilities give slopes \\(\\pm1\\), which are forbidden (they are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)). Hence the third line cannot be sunny; \\(k=0\\).\n\n   *\\(n=4\\).*  \n   Here \\(|T'_4|=3\\) and we have exactly two sunny lines to place. By Lemma 3 each sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T'_4\\); therefore to cover all three points at least one sunny line would have to contain two points. The three points of \\(T'_4\\) are \\((2,2),(2,3),(3,2)\\). Any line through two of them is either vertical, horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\); all three slopes are forbidden for a sunny line. Hence the two sunny lines can cover at most two points, leaving one uncovered – contradiction. Thus \\(k=0\\).\n\n   *\\(n=5\\).*  \n   Here \\(|T'_5|=6\\) and we have three sunny lines. Lemma 3 forces each sunny line to contain at most two points, so each must contain exactly two points. The six points of \\(T'_5\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   (2,2),(2,3),(2,4),\\;(3,2),(3,3),\\;(4,2).\n   \\]\n\n   Computing the slopes of all \\(\\binom{6}{2}=15\\) lines determined by two of these points shows that the only slopes not belonging to \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) are \\(-\\tfrac12\\) and \\(-2\\), each occurring for a single pai