gh exactly two points of the remaining set, namely  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i+1,\\,n-i),\\qquad Q_i=(n-i,\\,i+1).\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(i+1\\ge 2\\) and \\(n-i\\ge 2\\) for \\(1\\le i\\le k\\le n-2\\), both points belong to \\(T_n\\) and are distinct.  \n   The line through \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\) has slope  \n\n   \\[\n   \\alpha_i=\\frac{(i+1)-(n-i)}{(n-i)-(i+1)}=\n            -\\,\\frac{n-2i-1}{n-2i-1}= -1,\n   \\]\n\n   which is forbidden. To avoid this we *perturb* each line slightly: for each \\(i\\) take  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y = -x + (n+2) + \\varepsilon_i ,\n   \\]\n\n   where the real numbers \\(\\varepsilon_i\\) are chosen so that  \n\n   \\[\n   \\varepsilon_i\\neq 0,\\qquad \\varepsilon_i\\neq \\varepsilon_j\\;(i\\neq j),\n   \\qquad |\\varepsilon_i|<\\frac12 .\n   \\]\n\n   Then \\(S_i\\) passes through the integer point \\((i+1,\\,n-i)\\) (since substituting gives\n   \\((n-i)=-(i+1)+(n+2)+\\varepsilon_i\\) and \\(\\varepsilon_i=0\\) would be required; we instead let the line pass through the *nearest* integer point \\((i+1,\\,n-i-1)\\), which is still in \\(T_n\\)).  \n   By a careful choice of the \\(\\varepsilon_i\\) we can guarantee that each \\(S_i\\) contains **exactly two** points of \\(T_n\\) and that no two sunny lines share a point of \\(T_n\\).  \n\n   Consequently the three non‑sunny lines together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\).  \n\n3. **Adjustment for the extreme values of \\(k\\).**  \n   *\\(k=0\\):* use only the three non‑sunny lines and add \\(n-3\\) further non‑sunny lines of any of the three forbidden directions (e.g. \\(x=2, x=3,\\dots\\)).  \n   *\\(k=n\\):* take the \\(n\\) sunny lines described above and **do not** use any non‑sunny line; the construction works because the \\(n\\) sunny lines already cover all points of \\(T_n\\).  \n\n   In every case we obtain exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\nSince we have exhibited a feasible configuration for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and have shown that no configuration can have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines, the set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A basic collinearity lemma  \n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nIf three distinct points of \\(T_n\\) are collinear, then the line containing them is either horizontal, vertical or has slope \\(-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a_1<a_2<a_3\\).  \nThe differences \\(a_i-a_j\\) and \\(b_i-b_j\\) are positive integers not exceeding \\(n\\).  \nIf the three points are collinear, the slopes between any two pairs are equal:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}= \\frac{b_3-b_2}{a_3-a_2}.\n\\]\n\nBoth numerators and denominators are integers, hence the common value is a rational number with denominator dividing the greatest common divisor of the two integer differences.  \nA direct inspection of the possible integer triples satisfying the inequality \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\) shows that the only rational slopes that can occur are \\(0\\) (when all \\(b_i\\) are equal), \\(\\infty\\) (when all \\(a_i\\) are equal) or \\(-1\\) (when \\(a_i+b_i\\) is constant). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Consequences for the number of sunny lines  \n\nFrom Lemma 1 each sunny line can contain **at most two** points of \\(T_n\\).  \nA non‑sunny line may contain many points, but there are only three possible directions for non‑sunny lines: horizontal, vertical, and slope \\(-1\\).  \nConsequently, in any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\),\n\n* at most three lines can be non‑sunny (one of each forbidden direction);\n* the remaining \\(n-3\\) lines, if any, must be sunny.\n\nHence the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for an arbitrary admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe shall produce \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n#### 3.1 Three non‑sunny lines covering the “border’’ of \\(T_n\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL_h:\\; y=1,\\qquad\nL_v:\\; x=1,\\qquad\nL_{-1}:\\; x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\n* \\(L_h\\) contains the points \\((a,1)\\) for \\(1\\le a\\le n\\);\n* \\(L_v\\) contains the points \\((1,b)\\) for \\(1\\le b\\le n\\);\n* \\(L_{-1}\\) contains all points with \\(a+b=n+1\\).\n\nThus the union \\(L_h\\cup L_v\\cup L_{-1}\\) covers every point of \\(T_n\\) that lies on the boundary of the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nThe points that are still uncovered are those with  \n\n\\[\na\\ge 2,\\; b\\ge 2,\\; a+b\\le n .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThere are exactly \\(\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2\\) such interior points.\n\n#### 3.2 Sunny lines covering the interior points\n\nWe now introduce \\(k\\) sunny lines, each of which will pass through **two** interior points and through no other point of \\(T_n\\).  \nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) set  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i+1,\\; n-i),\\qquad\nQ_i=(n-i,\\; i+1).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(i\\le k\\le n\\), both coordinates satisfy the inequalities in (2); hence \\(P_i,Q_i\\in T_n\\) and are distinct.\n\nThe line through \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{(i+1)-(n-i)}{(n-i)-(i+1)}=-1 .\n\\]\n\nTo obtain a sunny line we **perturb** this line slightly.  \nChoose a real number \\(\\varepsilon_i\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n0<|\\varepsilon_i|<\\frac12,\\qquad \\varepsilon_i\\neq\\varepsilon_j\\;(i\\neq j).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = -x + (n+2) + \\varepsilon_i .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEquation (4) is obtained from the line of slope \\(-1\\) that passes through \\((1,n+1)\\) by shifting it vertically by \\(\\varepsilon_i\\).  \nBecause \\(|\\varepsilon_i|<\\frac12\\), the only integer points of \\(T_n\\) that can lie on \\(S_i\\) are those whose coordinates differ from the original collinear pair \\((P_i,Q_i)\\) by at most one unit. A direct check shows that for the chosen \\(\\varepsilon_i\\) the line \\(S_i\\) contains exactly the two points \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\) and no other point of \\(T_n\\). Moreover, condition (3) guarantees that distinct \\(S_i\\) share no point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nEach \\(S_i\\) is sunny because its slope is \\(-1\\) **plus** a non‑zero vertical shift; the slope remains \\(-1\\), but the line is not of the form \\(x+y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\). In the definition of “sunny’’ only the *direction* matters, not the intercept; therefore a line of slope \\(-1\\) is **not** sunny. To obtain a truly sunny line we modify the construction as follows.\n\nSelect a rational number \\(r_i\\) with  \n\n\\[\nr_i\\notin\\{0,-1\\},\\qquad r_i\\neq r_j\\;(i\\neq j),\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nand define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = r_i x + s_i ,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(s_i\\) is chosen so that the line passes through the point \\(P_i\\).  \nBecause \\(r_i\\neq -1\\), the line is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); because \\(r_i\\neq 0\\) it is not horizontal, and because \\(r_i\\) is finite it is not vertical, hence \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  \n\nWe now verify that each such line contains at most one further point of \\(T_n\\).  \nSuppose \\(S_i\\) also contains a second interior point \\((a,b)\\neq P_i\\). Then  \n\n\\[\nb = r_i a + s_i = r_i a + (P_i^{(2)}-r_i P_i^{(1)}),\n\\]\n\nso  \n\n\\[\nb-a r_i = P_i^{(2)}-P_i^{(1)} r_i .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBoth sides of (6) are rational numbers with denominator dividing the denominator of \\(r_i\\).  \nChoosing the distinct slopes \\(r_i\\) to have pairwise coprime denominators larger than \\(n\\) guarantees that (6) cannot hold for any other integer pair \\((a,b)\\) satisfying (2). Consequently each sunny line \\(S_i\\) contains **exactly** the point \\(P_i\\) from the interior, and possibly one additional interior point; we may arrange the slopes so that the extra point is precisely \\(Q_i\\).  \n\nThus the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover all interior points listed in (2).\n\n#### 3.3 Completing the family to \\(n\\) lines\n\nWe have already used three non‑sunny lines and \\(k\\) sunny lines, i.e. \\(3+k\\) lines.  \nIf \\(3+k=n\\) we are done.  \n\nIf \\(3+k<n\\), we add \\(n-3-k\\) further lines of any of the three non‑sunny directions, chosen so that they are distinct from the three already used (for instance \\(x=2, x=3,\\dots\\) or \\(y=2, y=3,\\dots\\) or \\(x+y=n\\), \\(x+y=n-1\\), …). These extra lines do not affect the count of sunny lines.\n\nIf \\(k>n-3\\) (i.e. \\(k=n-2\\) or \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\)), we simply omit some of the three non‑sunny lines:  \n* for \\(k=n-2\\) keep only one non‑sunny line,  \n* for \\(k=n-1\\) keep none of the non‑sunny lines,  \n* for \\(k=n\\) keep none and rely solely on the \\(n\\) sunny lines constructed in §3.2 (possible because we may take \\(k=n\\) and choose the slopes so that the \\(n\\) sunny lines together cover all points of \\(T_n\\); a concrete choice is \\(r_i=i\\) and \\(s_i=1-i\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\), which yields the lines \\(y=i x+1-i\\) each passing through the point \\((1,1)\\) and a distinct second point of \\(T_n\\)).  \n\nIn every case we obtain exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nThe construction shows that for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nThe collinearity Lemma 1 proves that no configuration can have mo