            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThat is, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, and no configuration can have a number of sunny lines outside this interval.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n2. **Upper bound.**  \n   *Lemma 1* (collinearity) shows that three points of \\(T_n\\) can be collinear only when the line is horizontal, vertical or has slope \\(-1\\).  \n   Consequently a sunny line can contain **at most two** points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Since there are only three possible directions for non‑sunny lines, at most three of the \\(n\\) lines can be non‑sunny. Hence the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le n .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Construction for an arbitrary admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   We treat three ranges of \\(k\\) separately.\n\n   *3.1  The case \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\).*  \n   Use the three non‑sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_h:\\;y=1,\\qquad L_v:\\;x=1,\\qquad L_{-1}:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   They cover every point of \\(T_n\\) that lies on the boundary of the triangle\n   \\(\\{a+b\\le n+1\\}\\).  \n   The remaining points are the *interior* points  \n\n   \\[\n   I_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n\\}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Write \\(I_n\\) as a disjoint union of the \\(n-3\\) vertical strips  \n\n   \\[\n   C_j=\\{(j+1,b)\\mid 2\\le b\\le n-j\\},\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,n-3 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   For each \\(j\\) choose a rational slope  \n\n   \\[\n   m_j=\\frac{1}{j}\\notin\\{0,-1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   and define the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_j:\\; y=m_jx+(2-m_j).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(x=j+1\\) into (5) gives \\(y=2\\); thus the point \\((j+1,2)\\in C_j\\) lies on \\(S_j\\).  \n   Because the slope is positive, the line \\(S_j\\) meets the strip \\(C_j\\) in **all** its points whose second coordinate runs from \\(2\\) up to \\(n-j\\); in particular every point of \\(C_j\\) belongs to \\(S_j\\).  \n   Distinct strips have distinct \\(x\\)‑coordinates, so the lines \\(S_j\\) are pairwise distinct and no point of \\(T_n\\) lies on two different \\(S_j\\).  \n\n   Hence the three non‑sunny lines (2) together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) cover all points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   To obtain exactly \\(n\\) lines we add \\(n-3-k\\) further non‑sunny lines of any of the three forbidden directions (for instance \\(x=2,x=3,\\dots\\)).  \n   The total number of sunny lines is precisely \\(k\\).\n\n   *3.2  The case \\(k=n-2\\).*  \n   Keep only one of the three non‑sunny lines, say \\(L_h\\).  \n   The interior points are now covered by the \\(n-2\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_{n-2}\\) defined in (5).  \n   Together we have \\(1+(n-2)=n-1\\) lines; we add one more non‑sunny line (e.g. \\(x=1\\)) to reach \\(n\\) lines.  \n   Exactly \\(n-2\\) of them are