hus for each admissible \\(k\\) a suitable family of \\(n\\) lines exists, proving that all integers \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) are attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound on \\(k\\)\n\nThere are exactly \\(n\\) lines in the required configuration, therefore the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).  We shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\\) satisfying the two conditions, with exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_{i}\\;:\\; x=i .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_{i}\\) is vertical, hence not sunny.  The points of \\(S\\) that lie on these lines are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-k,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The sunny lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{n-k+j}\\;:\\; y=\\frac{j}{\\,n+2-j\\,}x+\\frac{j}{\\,n+2-j\\,}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\dfrac{j}{n+2-j}\\) is positive and different from \\(0\\) and from \\(-1\\); consequently none of the lines (5) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  Hence each \\(L_{n-k+j}\\) is sunny.\n\nA direct substitution shows that a point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies (5) iff  \n\n\\[\nb=\\frac{j}{\\,n+2-j\\,}a+\\frac{j}{\\,n+2-j\\,}\n\\iff (n+2-j)b=j(a+1)\n\\iff a+b=n+2-j .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus the line \\(L_{n-k+j}\\) contains **exactly** the points of \\(S\\) whose coordinates sum to \\(n+2-j\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Partition of \\(S\\)\n\nThe set \\(S\\) can be written as a disjoint union  \n\n\\[\nS=\n\\Bigl(\\bigcup_{i=1}^{\\,n-k}\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}\\Bigr)\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\Bigl(\\bigcup_{j=1}^{\\,k}\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+2-j\\}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, the first union consists of all points with \\(a\\le n-k\\); the second union consists of all remaining points, each of which has a unique value of the sum \\(a+b\\) belonging to the set \\(\\{n+1, n, \\dots , n+2-k\\}\\).  Consequently every point of \\(S\\) lies on exactly one of the lines listed in (3)\u2013(5).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  Distinctness of the lines  \n\nThe vertical lines \\(x=i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n-k\\)) are clearly distinct.  \nFor the sunny lines, if \\(j\\neq j'\\) then the sums \\(n+2-j\\) and \\(n+2-j'\\) are different, so by (6) the two lines contain disjoint subsets of \\(S\\); consequently they are distinct.  Moreover a vertical line cannot coincide with a line of the form (5) because the latter has a non\u2011zero finite slope.  Hence all \\(n\\) lines are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Verification of the required properties  \n\n*Coverage.*  \nBy (7) each point of \\(S\\) belongs to one of the constructed lines, so condition (i) of the problem statement is satisfied.\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nExactly the \\(k\\) lines defined in (5) are sunny, while the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines (3) are not.  Hence condition (ii) holds.\n\nThus for the chosen \\(k\\) we have produced a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines meeting both requirements.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nSince the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), and no larger value of \\(k\\) is possible by (2), the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
