\n\\[\nA=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{\\,n-k}\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Justification:* For a fixed \\(i\\), the condition \\(a=i\\) together with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b\\le n+1-i\\); the lower bound \\(b\\ge1\\) is already required. Hence (4) correctly describes the points covered by the vertical lines.\n\nCardinality of \\(A\\):\n\n\\[\n|A|=\\sum_{i=1}^{\\,n-k}(n+1-i)=(n+1)(n-k)-\\frac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Verification:* The sum \\(\\sum_{i=1}^{m}(n+1-i)=m(n+1)-\\frac{m(m+1)}2\\) with \\(m=n-k\\) yields exactly (5). Thus (5) is correct.\n\n---\n\n##### 2.2 Sunny lines\n\nFor each \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\; y=\\frac{j}{\\,n+2-j\\,}x+\\frac{j}{\\,n+2-j\\,}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Slope analysis:* The slope \\(\\frac{j}{n+2-j}\\) is positive (both numerator and denominator are positive) and never equals \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\). Hence each \\(L_j\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore each \\(L_j\\) is sunny.\n\nEquivalence transformation:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nb&=\\frac{j}{\\,n+2-j\\,}a+\\frac{j}{\\,n+2-j\\,}\\\\\n\\iff (n+2-j)b&=j(a+1)\\\\\n\\iff a+b&=n+2-j .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Correctness:* Multiplying both sides of the first equality by \\(n+2-j\\) yields the second; rearranging gives the third. Hence (7) is valid.\n\nThus \\(L_j\\) contains exactly the points of \\(S\\) whose coordinates sum to \\(n+2-j\\). For a fixed \\(j\\) the number of such points equals  \n\n\\[\nn+1-j .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*Reasoning:* For a given sum \\(s=n+2-j\\), the admissible pairs \\((a,b)\\) satisfy \\(1\\le a\\le s-1\\) and \\(b=s-a\\). The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is automatically satisfied because \\(s=n+2-j\\le n+1\\). Hence there are \\(s-1=n+1-j\\) points, confirming (8).\n\n---\n\n##### 2.3 The remaining points\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nB=S\\setminus A .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nUsing (1) and (5),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n|B|&=|S|-|A|\\\\\n&=\\frac{n(n+1