2-\\Bigl[(n+1)(n-k)-\\frac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}2\\Bigr]\\\\\n&=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n*Verification:* Straightforward algebraic manipulation; the result matches the claimed expression.\n\nThe solution then argues that every point of \\(B\\) lies on one of the sunny lines \\(L_j\\). Indeed, for any \\((a,b)\\in B\\) we have \\(a>n-k\\) (otherwise it would belong to \\(A\\)). Consequently  \n\n\\[\na+b\\in\\{n+2,\\,n+1,\\dots ,n+2-k\\},\n\\]\n\nso there exists a unique \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) with \\(a+b=n+2-j\\). By (7) this point satisfies the equation of \\(L_j\\). Hence the claim is correct.\n\n---\n\n##### 2.4 Distinctness of the lines\n\n- The vertical lines \\(V_i\\) are clearly distinct because they have different \\(x\\)-coordinates.\n- The slopes \\(\\frac{j}{n+2-j}\\) are strictly increasing in \\(j\\) (both numerator and denominator increase, and the function is injective on the integer interval), so the lines \\(L_j\\) are pairwise distinct.\n- No vertical line can coincide with any \\(L_j\\) because each \\(L_j\\) has a finite non\u2011zero slope.\n\nThus the total collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k},L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\nAll statements in this subsection are justified.\n\n---\n\n#### 3. Verification of the required properties\n\n**Coverage.**  \n- If a point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) has \\(a\\le n-k\\), then by construction it belongs to \\(V_a\\) (see (4)).  \n- If \\(a>n-k\\), then \\((a,b)\\in B\\) and, as shown above, it lies on the sunny line \\(L_j\\) where \\(j=n+2-(a+b)\\).\n\nHence every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n**Number of sunny lines.**  \nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are sunny; the remaining \\(n-k\\) vertical lines are not. This matches the prescribed value of \\(k\\).\n\nBoth conditions of the problem are satisfied.\n\n---\n\n#### 4. Conclusion\n\nThe construction works for every integer \\(k