            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe number of points in \\(S\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound on \\(k\\)\n\nThere are exactly \\(n\\) lines in the required configuration, therefore  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction for a given \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Non‑sunny vertical lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are vertical, hence not sunny.  \nThe points of \\(S\\) that lie on the union of the \\(V_i\\)’s are  \n\n\\[\nA=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{\\,n-k}\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(A\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|A|=\\sum_{i=1}^{\\,n-k}(n+1-i)=(n+1)(n-k)-\\frac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Sunny lines  \n\nDefine, for each \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\),\n\n\\[\nL_j:\\; y=\\frac{j}{\\,n+2-j\\,}\\,x+\\frac{j}{\\,n+2-j\\,}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\dfrac{j}{n+2-j}\\) is positive and different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\); consequently each \\(L_j\\) is sunny.\n\nA point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies (6) iff  \n\n\\[\nb=\\frac{j}{\\,n+2-j\\,}a+\\frac{j}{\\,n+2-j\\,}\n\\iff (n+2-j)b=j(a+1)\n\\iff a+b=n+2-j .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(L_j\\) contains exactly the points of \\(S\\) whose coordinates sum to \\(n+2-j\\).  \nFor a fixed \\(j\\) the number of such points equals  \n\n\\[\nn+1-j .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  The remaining points  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nB=S\\setminus A .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nUsing (1) and (5),\n\n\\[\n|B|=|S|-|A|\n      =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\Bigl[(n+1)(n-k)-\\frac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}2\\Bigr]\n      =\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nFrom (7) we see that each diagonal \\(a+b=n+2-j\\) (with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\)) is a subset of \\(L_j\\).  \nAll points of \\(B\\) lie on one of these diagonals, because for any \\((a,b)\\in B\\) we have \\(a>n-k\\) and therefore  \n\n\\[\na+b\\in\\{n+2,\\,n+1,\\dots ,n+2-k\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. the sum equals \\(n+2-j\\) for a unique \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).  Consequently every point of \\(B\\) belongs to the sunny line \\(L_j\\) determined by its sum.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  Distinctness of the lines  \n\nThe vertical lines \\(V_i\\) are pairwise distinct.  \nThe slopes of the lines \\(L_j\\) are \\(\\dfrac{j}{n+2-j}\\) for \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\); these numbers are all different, so the \\(L_j\\) are pairwise distinct.  \nA vertical line cannot coincide with any \\(L_j\\) because the latter have non‑zero finite slope.  Hence all \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k},L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\n\\]\n\nare distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Verification of the required properties  \n\n*Coverage.*  \nEvery point of \\(S\\) with first coordinate \\(a\\le n-k\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\) (by (4)).  \nIf \\(a>n-k\\) then \\((a,b)\\in B\\) and, as shown in §2.3, it lies on the sunny line \\(L_j\\) where \\(j=n+2-(a+b)\\).  Hence each point of \\(S\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are sunny; the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are vertical and therefore not sunny.\n\nThus the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) satisfies both conditions of the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Conclusion  \n\nWe have exhibited, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), and in which exactly \\(k\\) lines are sunny.  Because obviously \\(k\\) cannot exceed \\(n\\), the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
